# H.A.T.E.: what the reverse knows about the game image $5FB4-$FFFF, one line an address.
# tools/mkgame.py turns it with the recordings (work/*/l1.json) into src/game/*.asm; the format is in its
# Annotations class. Code entries must be code the recordings ran or code reached only through a table.

@module buffers
$5FB4 b ProfileMargin 16 ; Bytes below the ground profile
> 76 bytes just below the ground profile $6000. In the image they hold mask/pixel byte pairs left from the build; the screen copy at $9F1C overwrites them from $BFB4-$BFFF (the image of $5B00-$5FFF) before play. The profile readers index from $5FF6-$5FF8 ($DA1E, $DAA1, $DB1B, $E3E5, $E94F), so the top of this block is the profile's left margin.
$6000 b ProfileBuffer 16 ; Ground profile buffer (768 bytes)
> The course's profile under the scroll, one byte for every 2 units along the course: the ground height (bits 0-4) and the walls (bits 5-7); docs/07-objects.md 7.4. Cleared at every round start ($CF68, $D0EF). In the image it holds leftovers of the build, and the entry $61A8 sits inside it: code that runs once and then becomes buffer space (docs/02-architecture.md 2.2).
$61A8 c Entry ; Game entry (RANDOMIZE USR 25000)
> The address BASIC line 30 calls. It sets the stack to the top of memory, disables interrupts and jumps to the start-up $9F12. Overwritten when the first round clears the profile buffer.
$61A8 ; the stack grows down from the top of RAM
$61AB ; the game proper runs with interrupts off
$61AC ; the start-up
$61AF b GroundProfileTail 16 ; Ground profile buffer, after the entry
> The rest of the profile buffer, to $62FF. The first 42 bytes are leftover loader code that never runs, the rest is zero.
$6300 b Font 8 ; Font, codes $20-$3A
> 64 glyphs of 8 bytes for codes $20-$5F at $6300-$64FF, glyph = $6300 + (code - $20) * 8; printed by $EE17 and, lines 2-6 only, by $EEA4 (docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.2). Codes $22-$2C are the big GAME OVER, $2E a red block, $2F a gauge segment. The glyph slots of ";" to "?" and "[" to "_" hold strings instead (next entries).
$63D8 t CreditsText1 ; Credits, first part (in the font's ";" to "?" slots)
> A print string for $EE17 (records: attribute + $80, column, row, characters; $FF ends): "WRITTEN BY", "COSTA PANAYI", "MUSIC BY" at column 1, rows 17-19, attribute $30. Printed by the menu $F1AA; docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.3.
$6400 b FontHigh 8 ; Font, codes $40-$5A
> The second half of the font: the (c) sign at code $40 ("@" in the strings) and the capitals.
$64D8 t CreditsText2 ; Credits, second part (in the font's "[" to "_" slots)
> "BEN DAGLISH", "(c)1989 VORTEX" at column 1, rows 20-21. Printed by the menu $F1AA.
$64F6 t AutoText ; " AUTO " in the top window
> Printed at (2,6) by the AUTO start in $E68C (docs/05-controls.md 5.4).
$6500 s SpriteScratch ; Visible-record list and sprite scratch page
> Work space of the sprite code. $EFA5 builds the list of visible records here each pass (pointer kept at $EF9F, $40 ends the list) and $F089 scans it; $DE08 fills $6500-$6543 with $FF as scratch. Zero in the image.
$6580 b SpriteGfx00 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $00
> The sprite graphics run from here to $7FFF. Each block is reached through the pointer table $C300 by a record's byte +8 (the low byte of the address into $C300): the player gets 2 x type ($D246 writes $C008), the templates $C2B4-$C2E1 and $C360/$C370 carry their own. The block boundaries below are the table's pointers.
$67F6 b SpriteGfx02 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $02
$6A6C b SpriteGfx04 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $04
$6CE2 b SpriteGfx06 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $06
$6F58 b SpriteGfx1C 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $1C
$70FC b SpriteGfx1E 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $1E
$72A0 b SpriteGfx14 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $14
$7416 b SpriteGfx08 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $08 (the type 4 missile template)
$758C b SpriteGfx0A 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slots $0A and $0C (the type 6 missile template)
$7702 b SpriteGfx0E 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $0E (the type 7 missile template)
$7878 b SpriteGfx10 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slots $10 and $12 (the jet templates)
$79EE b SpriteGfx16 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $16
$7AA4 b SpriteGfx18 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $18 (shots: templates $C360, $C2E1)
$7B5A b SpriteGfx1A 8 ; Sprite graphics, table slot $1A (the shell: template $C370)
$7C10 b ExplosionGfx22 8 ; Explosion frame, table slot $22
> The explosion: a hit record's byte +8 is set to $20 ($EBD1, $E262) and counts up by 2 a pass to $2C ($E235), so the frames are the slots $20-$2C; slot $20 and $24 share one picture.
$7CB8 b ExplosionGfx20 8 ; Explosion frame, table slots $20 and $24
$7D60 b ExplosionGfx26 8 ; Explosion frame, table slot $26
$7E08 b ExplosionGfx28 8 ; Explosion frame, table slot $28
$7EB0 b ExplosionGfx2A 8 ; Explosion frame, table slot $2A
$7F58 b ExplosionGfx2C 8 ; Explosion frame, table slot $2C (the last before the record is gone)
@module titleplayer
$8000 c TitlePlayer ; Title music player: relocating entry
> Entry of the title music player (Ben Daglish's beeper engine); docs/02-architecture.md 2.3, docs/09-sound.md. C = the tune number (the operand $8002, set to 0 by the start-up). It reads its own call address from the CALL operand below the return address, swaps $58C bytes from that address + $3A with $803A, calls the set-up $803A and swaps them back, so the player can be linked anywhere. Called as CALL $8000 the swap changes nothing. The same 64 pages are the playfield buffer $8000-$BFFF in play (docs/06-rendering.md 6.2).
$8001 ; the tune number: an operand written by the start-up ($9F16)
$8004 ; the return address: points after our CALL
$8006 ; DE = the CALL's operand, the address this copy was called at
$800A ; the player's body follows $3A bytes after the entry
$800F ; length of the body
$8015 SwapInLoop ; exchange (DE) and (HL), byte by byte
$8022 ; play; returns here on the tune's end or a key
$8025 c TitlePlayerSwapBack ; Title music player: swap the body back
> Undoes the swap of $8000 and returns to the start-up.
$802C SwapBackLoop ; exchange the bytes again
$803A c TitleSetup ; Title player set-up: hook IM 2, reset the voices, start the tune
> Saves SP into the operand at $8364 (for the exits at $8356), points SP at its own small stack below $83F7, reads the IM 2 vector address from I (the vector table byte at I*256, doubled: $FDFD) and saves the 3 bytes there to $83F9, then writes JP $8340 in their place. Applies the 9 (address, byte) patches of $8422 to reset its own operands, enables interrupts, and starts tune C from the table $843D; docs/02-architecture.md 2.3.
$803A ; saved for the exits: the operand of LD SP,nn at $8363
$803E ; the player's own stack, $83E2-$83F6
$8041 ; I = the high byte of the vector table
$8046 ; D = E = the table byte: the vector address is $FDFD
$8049 ; the vector address, used again by the exit $8356
$8052 ; keep the 3 bytes found at the vector
$8054 ; JP $8340, written backwards over the vector
$805E ; nine patches
$8063 PatchLoop ; (DE) = the patch byte
$806D ; wait for the first interrupt: start on a frame
$806F ; HL = the song of tune C
$807C ; C = 0: no note sounding yet
$807F c NoteTimeCheck ; Title player: is the current note over?
> Entered from the voice loop after each period of voice B. C counts the note's frames down (the IM 2 handler decrements it); while it is not 0 the voices go on, at 0 the next pattern command is read.
$807F ; C: frames left of the note
$8084 c PatternStep ; Title player: next note, reload the note length
> Reloads C with the note length (the operand $8085, set by command 17), then reads the next command of the pattern.
$8084 ; the note length, an operand written by command 17 ($80F8)
$8087 ; HL = the pattern position, kept in DE' between notes
$8088 c PatternCommand ; Title player: dispatch one pattern command
> Reads the command byte at HL and jumps through the handler table $83FC (2 bytes a command) with the position after the byte on the stack; docs/09-sound.md 9.3.
$808E ; the handler pops the position
$808F ; the command table
$8097 ; to the command's handler
$8098 c CmdWidthA ; Title command 1: voice A's starting pulse width
> Writes the argument byte into the operands at $81DE and $8205, the LD B,n of voice A's note set-up in commands 10 and 11: the pulse width a note starts with, its loudness.
$809B ; voice A, command 10's set-up
$809E ; voice A, command 11's set-up
$80A4 c CmdWidthB ; Title command 2: voice B's starting pulse width
> Writes the argument byte into the operands at $81BB and $8231, the LD B,n of voice B's note set-up in commands 10 and 12.
$80A7 ; voice B, command 10's set-up
$80AA ; voice B, command 12's set-up
$80B0 c CmdEnvelopeA ; Title command 3: envelope step time of voice A
> Writes the argument into the operand at $827E, the reload of H (voice A's envelope frame counter) when a note starts.
$80B3 ; read by $81E7 and $8213 into H
$80B9 c CmdEnvelopeB ; Title command 4: envelope step time of voice B
> Writes the argument into the operand at $82E0, the reload of L (voice B's envelope frame counter) when a note starts.
$80BC ; read by $81CC and $823F into L
$80C2 b CmdModeA ; Title commands 5 and 6: envelope mode of voice A (not run)
> Two handlers that did not run in the recording: POP HL; LD B,1; JP $8088 (command 5, mode 1: hold) and POP HL; LD B,0; JP $8088 (command 6, mode 0: decay). B of the pattern's register set is voice A's envelope mode; docs/09-sound.md 9.4.
$80CE c CmdModeBHold ; Title command 7: voice B envelope mode 1 (hold)
> C of the pattern's register set is voice B's envelope mode.
$80D4 c CmdModeBDecay ; Title command 8: voice B envelope mode 0 (decay)
> Sets voice B's envelope mode C = 0 and goes back to the pattern reader (PatternCommand); docs/09-sound.md 9.3.
$80DA b CmdSilence ; Title commands 13-15: silence the voices (not run)
> Three handlers that did not run in the recording. Each zeroes an OUT value operand: command 13 at $80DA both ($82D0 and $8332), command 14 at $80E5 voice A ($82D0), command 15 at $80ED voice B ($8332); then JP $8088.
$80F5 c CmdNoteLength ; Title command 17: set the note length
> The argument goes into the operand of LD C,n at $8085 and into C' at once.
$80F8 ; the reload at $8084
$8101 c CmdDrums ; Title command 16: change the drum pattern from a pattern
> Starts the drum pattern whose address follows the command byte ($815E), then reads the next command.
$8109 c CmdPatternEnd ; Title command 9: end of pattern
> Counts the repeats down (the operand $810E). While some remain, it restarts the pattern from its start (the operand $810B); otherwise it goes back to the song at the position saved in $8116; docs/09-sound.md 9.3.
$810A ; the pattern's start, an operand written by $8152
$810D ; repeats left, an operand written by $8110 and $8146
$8113 ; again: restart the pattern
$8115 ; the song position, an operand written by $814E
$8118 c SongStep ; Title player: interpret the song
> Reads one song byte at HL: $FF return from a sub-song, $FE call a sub-song, $FD jump, $FC end of the tune, $FB start a drum pattern, n < $FB play the pattern at the following address n times; docs/09-sound.md 9.2.
$811A ; not $FF
$811C b SongReturn ; Title song byte $FF: return (not run)
> LD HL,nn; JP $8118: back to the position saved by $FE (the operand $811D). The tune of this game uses no sub-song.
$8122 c SongCallCheck ; Title player: song byte $FE?
> Song bytes from $FE down: $FE calls a sub-song (SongCall, not run), anything else is a jump in the song (SongJump).
$8123 ; not $FE
$8125 b SongCall ; Title song byte $FE: call a sub-song (not run)
> INC HL; LD E,(HL); INC HL; LD D,(HL); INC HL; LD ($811D),HL; EX DE,HL; JP $8118: saves the position after the address into SongReturn's operand and continues at the address.
$8131 c SongJump ; Title player: song bytes $FD, $FC, $FB and patterns
> $FD jumps to the address that follows (tune 0 loops this way); $FC leaves through $8356, the same exit as a key; $FB starts a drum pattern; any other byte n plays the pattern at the following address n times: n goes into $810E, the pattern's start into $810B and the song position after it into $8116.
$813C SongEndOrDrums ; $FC, $FB or a pattern
$813D ; $FC: the end of the tune
$8141 ; $FB: drums
$8144 ; n - 1 more repeats after this one
$8146 ; repeats, the operand of LD A,n at $810D
$814E ; the song position, the operand at $8115
$8152 ; the pattern's start, the operand at $810A
$8156 c PatternEnter ; Title player: enter a pattern
> Back to the pattern register set, where DE holds the pattern position, then $8084.
$815A c SongDrums ; Title song byte $FB: start a drum pattern
> Calls DrumStart with the return set to $8118, the next song byte.
$815E c DrumStart ; Title player: start the drum pattern at the address after HL
> Clears the drum timer (operand $8244), stores the pattern's first byte, its speed in frames, into the operand $824B, and its step address into $8250 (the position) and $8258 (the loop point); docs/09-sound.md 9.5.
$815F ; drum timer = 0: the first step at once
$8168 ; the speed: frames a step
$816C ; the loop point, used by drum byte 1
$8170 ; the position
$8175 c CmdHold ; Title command 0: hold the notes one more note length
> Command 0: nothing changes this note length; the player goes on to the drum tick (DrumTick) with the notes as they are.
$817A c CmdLongNote ; Title command 18: hold the notes n note lengths
> The counter is its own operand at $817B: 0 means the command starts, it loads n from the argument; otherwise it counts down, staying on this command until 0, then steps over the argument.
$817A ; the counter, an operand: 0 = not counting
$8183 ; one note length done
$8187 ; more to go: keep the notes
$8191 LongNoteStart ; the argument n: the counter
$8193 ; start counting
$819B c CmdNoteAB ; Title command 10: a note on both voices
> Two period bytes: voice A's goes into D of the voice loop and the reload operand $82CA, voice B's into E and $832C. Each voice's pulse width ($82D4, $8336) starts at the width of command 1 or 2, its pre-delay ($82CC, $832E) is period / 8 + $18 less that width, and its OUT value ($82D0, $8332) is $18 (beeper on); H and L get the envelope times of commands 3 and 4. When an envelope mode is not 0, the note starts mode 3; docs/09-sound.md 9.3-9.4.
$819D ; voice A in mode 0: leave it
$819F b NoteABModeA ; Title command 10: voice A mode 3 (not run)
> LD B,3: voice A starts in mode 3 when its mode is not 0. Voice A was in mode 0 through the recording.
$81A1 c CmdNoteABVoiceB ; Title command 10: voice B mode and the periods
> Command 10: voice B starts in envelope mode 3 when C is not 0; then both periods from the pattern (NoteABPeriods), voice B's written into its period reload VoiceBStep+$0005.
$81A5 ; voice B starts in mode 3
$81A7 NoteABPeriods ; the two periods
$81AF ; voice B's period reload
$81BA ; voice B's starting width, an operand written by command 2
$81BD ; voice B's pre-delay: period / 8 + $18 - width
$81C1 ; voice B's pulse width
$81C6 ; voice B on
$81C9 ; voice A on
$81CC ; L = voice B's envelope time
$81D2 ; voice A's period reload
$81DD ; voice A's starting width, an operand written by command 1
$81E0 ; voice A's pre-delay
$81E4 ; voice A's pulse width
$81E7 ; H = voice A's envelope time
$81EE c CmdNoteA ; Title command 11: a note on voice A only
> One period byte for voice A, set up as in command 10.
$81F0 ; mode 0: leave it
$81F2 b NoteAModeA ; Title command 11: voice A mode 3 (not run)
> LD B,3, as in command 10.
$81F4 c CmdNoteAPeriod ; Title command 11: voice A's period
> Command 11: voice A's period from the pattern into its reload VoiceAStep+$0005, and its pre-delay VoiceAStep+$0007 = period / 8 + $18 - the starting width (an operand command 1 writes); docs/09-sound.md 9.3.
$81F9 ; voice A's period reload
$8204 ; voice A's starting width, an operand written by command 1
$8207 ; voice A's pre-delay
$820B ; voice A's pulse width
$8210 ; voice A on
$821A c CmdNoteB ; Title command 12: a note on voice B only
> One period byte for voice B, set up as in command 10.
$821E ; voice B starts in mode 3
$8220 NoteBPeriod ; the period
$8225 ; voice B's period reload
$8230 ; voice B's starting width, an operand written by command 2
$8233 ; voice B's pre-delay
$8237 ; voice B's pulse width
$823C ; voice B on
$8243 c DrumTick ; Title player: drum timer, then the voices
> The operand $8244 is the drum timer, decremented once a frame by the IM 2 handler. At 0 it is reloaded from the speed (operand $824B) and the next drum byte is played: 0 rest, 1 loop to the pattern's start, 2-6 the sounds $8367, $837F, $839A, $83C6, $83B0; docs/09-sound.md 9.5.
$8243 ; the drum timer, an operand the IM 2 handler decrements
$824A ; the speed, an operand written by $8168
$824F ; the drum position, an operand written by $8170 and $8261
$8252 DrumByte ; the next drum byte
$8254 ; not 1: a sound or a rest
$8257 ; the loop point, an operand written by $816C
$8260 DrumSound ; play byte 2-6; 0 falls through to the voices
$8279 c VoiceAEnvelope ; Title player: voice A's envelope tick
> H counts voice A's envelope frames (the IM 2 handler decrements it). At 0 it is reloaded with 3 and voice A's envelope mode B' is applied: 0 decays the note ($82A3), 1-3 are handled by the bytes $8284 (not run in the recording).
$827B ; not yet: play the voices
$827D ; an operand written by command 3
$8282 ; mode 0: decay
$8284 b VoiceAModes ; Title player: voice A envelope modes 1-3 (not run)
> DEC A; JR Z,$82BD (mode 1: hold); DEC A; JR Z,$8296 (mode 2: B = 3, voice on, the period reloaded, then a decay step); mode 3: B = 2, voice off ($82D0 = 0), H = 1, JP $82C1. Modes 2 and 3 alternate: a stutter. Voice B's copy of this code ran ($82E2).
$82A3 c VoiceADecay ; Title player: voice A's decay step
> Narrows voice A's pulse ($82D4) and lengthens its pre-delay ($82CC) by one: the same pitch, quieter. When the width runs out the bytes at $82B1 silence the voice.
$82A3 ; voice A's pulse width
$82A9 ; voice A's pre-delay
$82B1 b VoiceAOff ; Title player: voice A's pulse width ran out (not run)
> INC (HL) (the width back to 1); XOR A; LD ($82D0),A (voice A off); LD A,B; AND A; JP Z,$82BD; LD B,1 (a mode other than 0 becomes hold); $82BD: EXX; JP $82C5.
$82C1 c VoicesPlay ; Title player: the two voices
> One period step of each voice. D counts voice A's period down; at 0 it is reloaded (the operand $82CA) and one pulse is sent: pre-delay ($82CC), beeper on ($82D0, $18), pulse width ($82D4), beeper off. Then voice B's envelope tick (L, mode C') and the same for voice B with E at $8327; docs/09-sound.md 9.4.
$82C1 ; balance the time of the envelope branch
$82C3 BalanceDelayLoop
$82C5 VoiceAStep ; voice A's period counter
$82C9 ; voice A's period, an operand written by the note commands
$82CB ; voice A's pre-delay, an operand
$82CD VoiceAPreDelay
$82CF ; voice A's OUT value: $18 on, 0 silenced
$82D1 ; beeper on, border black
$82D3 ; voice A's pulse width, an operand
$82D5 VoiceAPulse
$82D9 ; beeper off
$82DB VoiceBEnvelope ; L: voice B's envelope frames
$82DF ; an operand written by command 4
$82E2 ; C: voice B's envelope mode
$82E4 ; mode 0: decay
$82E7 ; mode 1: hold
$82EA ; mode 2
$82EC ; mode 3: the voice off for one tick, then mode 2
$82F8 VoiceBModeOn ; mode 2: the voice on, the period restarted, then mode 3
$8305 VoiceBDecay ; voice B's pulse width
$830B ; voice B's pre-delay
$8313 b VoiceBOff ; Title player: voice B's pulse width ran out (not run)
> INC (HL); XOR A; LD ($8332),A (voice B off); LD A,C; AND A; JP Z,$831F; LD C,1; $831F: EXX; JP $8327. The JR Z,$831F at $82E7 (mode 1) enters at its tail.
$8323 c VoiceBDelay ; Title player: balance the envelope branch
> A 4-step delay loop that makes the envelope branch take as long as the other one, so voice B's period step comes at the same time either way.
$8325 c VoiceBDelayLoop ; Title player: delay loop
> The delay loop of VoiceBDelay (B = 4), then voice B's period step.
$8327 c VoiceBStep ; Title player: voice B's period step
> E counts voice B's period down; at 0 one pulse is sent like voice A's. Then back to $807F.
$832B ; voice B's period, an operand written by the note commands
$832D ; voice B's pre-delay, an operand
$832F VoiceBPreDelay
$8331 ; voice B's OUT value: $18 on, 0 silenced
$8333 ; beeper on
$8335 ; voice B's pulse width, an operand
$8337 VoiceBPulse
$833B ; beeper off
$8340 c TitleInterrupt ; Title player: the IM 2 handler, once a frame
> Reached through JP $8340 at $FDFD. A key on any row ends the music ($8356). Otherwise it ticks the engine's clocks: the drum timer ($8244) and the registers of the interrupted voice loop, C (the note length), H and L (the envelopes); 123 T an entry. docs/02-architecture.md 2.3.
$8343 ; A = 0: all keyboard rows
$8348 ; a key: leave the music
$834B ; the drum timer
$8350 ; the note length
$8351 ; voice A's envelope
$8352 ; voice B's envelope
$8356 TitleExit ; the tune ends ($FC) or a key: unhook and unwind
$8357 ; the vector address saved at $8049
$835B ; put back the 3 bytes the set-up replaced
$8363 ; SP as $803A saved it: the operand $8364
$8366 ; back into $8025, at any depth of the player
$8367 c DrumClick ; Title drum 2: a click
> The beeper held on for 300 loop steps, then a pause; docs/09-sound.md 9.5.
$836A ; beeper on
$836F ClickHoldLoop
$8375 ; A = 0 here: beeper off
$8379 ClickPauseLoop
$837F c DrumNoise ; Title drum 3: noise from the ROM
> 150 pulses whose levels and lengths are the bytes of the ROM from $0064: code read as random numbers.
$8382 ; the ROM as a noise table
$8385 NoisePulseLoop
$8389 ; a level from one byte
$838B ; a length from the next
$8390 NoiseLengthLoop
$839A c DrumRise ; Title drum 4: a rising tone, half-period 50 down to 1
> The beeper toggled 50 times with two waits of D each, D from 50 down to 1: a tone rising in pitch. Then back to voice A's envelope.
$839E RiseStepLoop
$839F RiseFirstWait
$83A2 RiseSecondWait
$83A6 ; toggle the beeper
$83B0 c DrumFall ; Title drum 6: a falling tone, period 180 up in steps of 4
> The beeper toggled with one wait of D, D from 180 up in steps of 4 until it wraps past 255 (19 steps): a tone falling in pitch.
$83B4 FallStepLoop
$83B5 FallWait
$83B9 ; toggle the beeper
$83C6 c DrumSweep ; Title drum 5: a rising tone, period 200 down to 124
> The beeper toggled with one wait of D, D from 200 down in steps of 4 while it is at least 124 (20 steps): a short rising tone, then back to voice A's envelope.
$83CB SweepStepLoop
$83CD SweepWait
$83D1 ; toggle the beeper
$83E2 b TitleStack ; Title player's stack
> SP points at $83F7 from $803E on: the interrupts and the command dispatch push here. Stale bytes in the image.
$83F7 w TitleVectorAddr 1 ; The IM 2 vector address
> Written at $8049 ($FDFD when I = $FE), read by the exit $8357. The image holds a stale value.
$83F9 b TitleVectorSave 3 ; The 3 bytes the title replaced at the vector
> Saved at $8052, put back at $8361.
$83FC w TitleCommands 4 ; Pattern command handlers 0-18
> Read by $8088, 2 bytes a command: 0 $8175 hold, 1-4 $8098-$80B9 voice parameters, 5-8 $80C2-$80D4 envelope modes, 9 $8109 end of pattern, 10-12 $819B, $81EE, $821A notes, 13-15 $80DA-$80ED silence, 16 $8101 drums, 17 $80F5 note length, 18 $817A long note; docs/09-sound.md 9.3.
$8422 b TitlePatches 3 ; Patch list: 9 x (address, byte)
> Applied by $803A at every start: periods $64 ($82CA, $832C), both voices silenced ($82D0, $8332), pulse widths $17 ($82D4, $8336), pre-delays 1 ($82CC, $832E), the long-note counter 0 ($817B).
$843D w TuneTable 1 ; Tune table
> One song address a tune, picked by C at $806F. Tune 0 is $843F.
$843F b TuneSong 3 ; Tune 0: the song
> Song entries ($8118): n and a pattern address, $FB and a drum pattern address, $FD and an address (jump). It plays $8467 once, then $8473 x5, $84A8, $84C6 x2, $853B, $84C6 x2, $853B with drums $85A2 and $85B4, and jumps back to the start at $8460: the tune loops until a key. The last 4 bytes (pattern $83E8, $FC) are never reached.
$8467 b PatternInit 4 ; Tune 0: set-up pattern
> Command 8 (voice B mode 0), 17 (note length 5), 1-4 (the voices' parameters), 9 (end). Pattern commands are listed at $83FC.
$8473 b PatternA 4 ; Tune 0: pattern A
> Notes: command 10 with two periods, 11 or 12 with one; 0 holds, 18 n holds n note lengths, 9 ends the pattern.
$84A8 b PatternB 4 ; Tune 0: pattern B
$84C6 b PatternC 4 ; Tune 0: pattern C
$853B b PatternD 4 ; Tune 0: pattern D
> Uses command 16 at $8583 to switch to the drums $85B4.
$85A2 b DrumPatternA 18 ; Drum pattern: clicks and noise
> The speed (5 frames a step), then the steps: 2 click, 3 noise, 0 rest, 1 loop; docs/09-sound.md 9.5.
$85B4 b DrumPatternB 18 ; Drum pattern: the three tones
> Speed 5, then the drums 4 (rising), 5 (sweep) and 6 (falling) and 1 loop.
$85C6 s TitleTail ; Unused, zero
@module startup
$8600 b StartupCopy 8 ; A stale copy of $9F00-$9F69
> Never run. The bytes equal the start-up block $9F00-$9F69 (with its PRESS ANY KEY string at $862F): a copy left $1900 bytes below its place. The whole of $8700-$9FFF repeats $6E00-$86FF this way (next entries).
$866A s StartupCopyTail ; Unused, zero
$8700 b SpriteGfxCopy 8 ; A stale copy of the sprite graphics $6E00-$7FFF
> Byte for byte the same as $6E00-$7FFF. The start-up head at $9F00 (not run in this version) moves $7C00-$9FFF down to $6300, which would put this copy in place; here the data is already there. Overwritten by the playfield in play.
$9900 b TitlePlayerCopy 8 ; A stale copy of the title player $8000-$85FF
> The same bytes as $8000-$85FF except the player's self-modified operands, which are 0 here ($9985 for $8085, $9C64 for $8364 and others): the player as assembled, before a run. The copy at $8000 carries the values of a run.
$9F00 b StartupHead 9 ; Start-up head (not run)
> DI; LD HL,$7C00; LD DE,$6300; LD BC,$2400; LDIR (move the font and graphics down to their place) then LD HL,$9F2F; CALL $EE17 (print PRESS ANY KEY). The game enters at $9F12, after these bytes; docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.3.
$9F12 c Startup ; Start-up: IM 2, the title music, then the game
> Called once from the entry $61A8. Sets IM 2 ($9F4B), plays tune 0 of the title player until it ends or a key is pressed, then copies the game screen image $A000-$BFFF to $4000-$5FFF, clears the lap $C2F8 and enters the game through $C2B1; docs/02-architecture.md 2.3.
$9F15 ; tune 0
$9F16 ; into the operand of LD C,n at $8001
$9F19 ; the title music
$9F1C StartGame ; the music is over
$9F1D ; the game screen image: pixels, attributes and $5B00-$5FFF
$9F29 ; lap 0
$9F2C ; the main loop, interrupts off from here on
$9F2F t PressKeyText ; "PRESS ANY KEY WHEN READY"
> A print string for $EE17 at (4,23), attribute 7. Only the start-up head $9F00 and its copy print it; neither ran.
$9F4B c SetupIM2 ; Set up IM 2 with a safe vector
> Writes JP $9F68 at $FDFD, fills the 257 bytes $FE00-$FF00 with $FD and sets I = $FE, IM 2: whatever the bus holds, the vector is $FDFD; docs/02-architecture.md 2.3.
$9F4B ; JP opcode
$9F50 ; the default handler: EI; RET
$9F59 ; B = 0: 256 bytes, C = the fill byte $FD
$9F5C FillVectorTable
$9F60 ; the 257th byte
$9F61 ; the vector table's page
$9F68 b DefaultInterrupt ; Default IM 2 handler: EI; RET
> The target of JP $FDFD until the title player hooks it. Data in the recording: it never ran.
$9F6A s StartupPad ; Unused, zero
$A000 b GameScreenImage 16 ; Game screen image: pixels
> The play screen with its panel, copied to $4000 by $9F1C when play starts ($A000-$B7FF the pixels). Part of the playfield buffer afterwards.
$B800 b GameScreenAttrs 16 ; Game screen image: attributes
> Copied to $5800-$5AFF.
$BB00 b RendererTablesImage 8 ; Image of $5B00-$5FFF: the renderer's tables
> The last 1 280 bytes of the copy land at $5B00-$5FFF. The renderer reads them there: $5B00, $5B20, $5B40 ($E011-$E01E), $5B60, $5C60, $5BE0 ($DF23-$DF31) and $5DE8, $5EF0, $5CE0 ($DE1C-$DE29). $BFB4-$BFFF become ProfileMargin.
@module gamedata
$C000 b PlayerRecord 16 ; Object record: the player
> Every moving or hittable thing is a 16-byte record in the list $C000-$C26F, ended by $FF at $C270; docs/07-objects.md 7.1-7.2. +0 flags and type (bit 7 gone, 6 end, 5 hit, 0-3 type), +1/+2 position along, +3 across, +4 height, +5 direction, +6 speed, +7 behaviour, +8 the sprite slot in $C300 (also the explosion frame), +9-+B screen place, +C/+E motion steps, +F fire timer. Zero in the image: the round start fills the list. Bit 7 of $C000 set is a lost life ($CFEF).
$C010 b PlayerShotRecords 16 ; Object records: the player's shots (5)
> $E82F fires into a free one; $CFD6 counts the free ones for the gauge.
$C060 b PodRecords 16 ; Object records: pods (4)
> Released by shot domes ($EDFE); docs/07-objects.md 7.5.
$C0A0 b ParkedVehicleRecord 16 ; Object record: the vehicle of the other kind
> Parked at position $600, from VehicleTemplate ($D1FB); the vehicle of the next round.
$C0B0 b CourseObjectRecords 16 ; Object records: the course's objects (16)
> The round's block of the course data, 8 bytes each, the rest zeroed ($D250).
$C1B0 b LaunchedRecords 16 ; Object records: launched missiles and jets (6)
> Filled from the templates $C2B4 by a firing object ($E1DD).
$C210 b EnemyShotRecords 16 ; Object records: the enemy's shots (5)
> Filled from EnemyShotTemplate ($E1E1).
$C260 b PreviousVehicleRecord 16 ; Object record: the vehicle of the round before
> At position $6C ($D27A).
$C270 b ObjectListEnd ; End of the object list ($FF)
$C271 b PassCounter ; Pass counter of the mover
> Incremented once a pass by $E15C; steering acts when its low 2 bits are 0 (every 4 passes).
$C272 b HeightWave 16 ; Height wave: 16 steps
> Followed by records with behaviour bit 1 ($E3BF), index +E mod 16.
$C282 b MissileArc 13 ; Height arc of a missile or shell
> 9 up to 24 and down to 2, then $FF: a height of 25 or more ends the flight ($E38F). The shell's start is found in it from the player's height ($E878).
$C28F b InputDevice ; Input device: 0 keyboard, 1 Sinclair, 2 Kempston, 3 Protek-AGF
> Set by the menu ($F26D), read by the device reader ($E6FF); docs/05-controls.md 5.1.
$C290 b ControlByte ; Control byte: bit 0 left, 1 right, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire
> Written once a pass by the device reader $E68C; docs/05-controls.md 5.2. The byte after it ($C291) is not referenced by address.
$C292 b PlayerTemplate 7 ; Player start: +1 to +7 of $C000
> Position along $0080, across $2C, height 2, direction 0, speed 1, behaviour 0; copied by $D1FB at each round start.
$C299 s UnusedC299 ; Not referenced by address
$C2A1 b VehicleTemplate 16 ; The parked and the previous vehicle records
> $C2A1 is set to the type of the round's vehicle ($D1FB) and becomes +0 of $C260 in the next round ($D27A). $C2A2-$C2B0 are copied to $C0A1 (position along $0600, across $2C, height 2); $C2A4-$C2B0 to $C263.
$C2B1 c GameStart ; Game start: JP to the main loop
> Entered once from the start-up $9F2C.
$C2B4 b LaunchTemplates 9 ; Templates of launched objects (5 x 9 bytes)
> +0 to +8 of a record (the position is then copied from the firer, the rest cleared): type 8 jet, type 7 missile (written at $D1FB: course vehicle type + 6), type 8 jet, type 4 missile, type 6 missile. A pad picks one by the highest set bit of its behaviour bits 7-4 ($E1D7); the dome of type 2 throws out the third ($E259); docs/07-objects.md 7.4.
$C2E1 b EnemyShotTemplate 9 ; Template of an enemy shot
> Type 12, direction 4, speed 4, sprite slot $18; used by firing objects below type 14 ($E1BD).
$C2EA w MoverAlong 1 ; New position along of the record being moved
> Written by $E2D7 (position + direction step x speed), read by the hit window set-up $E531.
$C2EC w HitWindowAlong 1 ; Hit test window starts, along (3 words)
> The moving record's new position along less half the box, for the three size classes of the other record; set at $E531, compared in $E586-$E62D; docs/07-objects.md 7.4.
$C2F2 b MoverAcross ; New position across of the record being moved
$C2F3 b HitWindowAcross ; Hit test window starts, across (3 bytes)
$C2F6 b MoverHeight ; New height of the record being moved
> docs/10-gameplay-logic.md; the terrain check $E400-$E43B corrects it.
$C2F7 b RoundNumber ; Round number, 1-10
> docs/04-game-loop.md 4.2.
$C2F8 b LapNumber ; Lap: passes through the 10 rounds
> 0 at the start ($9F29); from 1 on the course is armed harder ($D296, docs/07-objects.md 7.8).
$C2F9 b Lives ; Lives of the round
$C2FA t RoundText ; Round number string: two digits at (21,18)
> Printed by $D1BF; the digits are written into $C2FD-$C2FE (the record: $87, $15, $12, digit, digit, $FF).
$C300 w SpriteTable 4 ; Sprite graphics pointers
> 23 addresses into $6580-$7FFF, read as $C300 + record byte +8 ($DBEC, $DC67, $DCD6, $EA8F). Slots $20-$2C are the explosion frames.
$C32E b ScrollPage ; Scroll state: page step 0-63
> docs/06-rendering.md 6.2-6.4.
$C32F b ScrollHalf ; Scroll state: strip half, 0 or $80
$C330 b UnusedC330 ; Not referenced by address
$C331 w SavedStack 1 ; SP saved by the blit and the strip builder
> $D35E and $D536 use SP to walk address lists.
$C333 t ShotGaugeText ; Shot gauge string
> Five cells at (3,6), filled with "/" for each free shot slot by $CFD6, then a "/" at (2,6); printed at $CFE9; docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.3.
$C340 w Directions 2 ; Direction steps: along, across (8 x 4 bytes)
> Read by $E2B3: the word +0 is the step along, the byte +2 the step across; docs/07-objects.md 7.1.
$C360 b ShotTemplate 16 ; Template of the player's shot
> Type 12, direction 0, speed 6, sprite slot $18; copied by $E889.
$C370 b ShellTemplate 16 ; Template of the ground vehicle's shell
> Type 12, speed 6, behaviour bit 2 (the arc $C282), sprite slot $1A; copied by $E841.
$C380 w HitHandlers 8 ; Hit handlers: 7 rows of 8
> $EBAA jumps to the word at $C380 + 16 x (moving type / 2) + 2 x (other type / 2). Rows for the moving types 0-1 to 12-13; types 14-15 never move first. $EBD1 both hit, $EC0B nothing, $EC0F both turn, $EC27 player meets a shot, $E57E nothing (next pair), the rest score or collect; docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$C3F0 b WeavePattern 8 ; Weave: direction sequence 4 5 6 5 4 3 2 3
> Behaviour bit 6 ($E327), index +C mod 8.
$C3F8 w CourseRowPtr 1 ; Next row of the expanded course
> docs/06-rendering.md 6.5; $D49C, $D536.
$C3FA w StripCount 1 ; Strips drawn in the round
> At 32 the round starts ($D4BF).
$C3FC w FreshStripPage 1 ; Page of the last fresh strip
> Written by $D57F; docs/06-rendering.md 6.4.
$C3FE s UnusedC3FE ; Not referenced by address

@module course
$C400 b Tiles 8 ; Course tiles 0-22, 8 x 8 bytes
> The tile set of the course: 72 tiles of 8 bytes at $C400 + 8 x tile, one byte (8 pixels) a line, the first byte at the bottom of the strip. FreshStrip ($D51D) copies 15 of them into each fresh strip (docs/06-rendering.md 6.5). The tiles are split here only to name the two edge tiles 23 and 24.
$C4B8 b EdgeTiles 8 ; Edge tiles 23 and 24
> The first 8 lines of every fresh strip: tile 23 ($C4B8) for three strips in four, tile 24 ($C4C0) for the fourth (FreshStrip, $D534). No tile run uses them.
$C4C8 b TilesHigh 8 ; Course tiles 25-71
> The rest of the tile set (see Tiles).
$C640 b TileRuns 16 ; Runs of tile numbers, one per tile across the track
> A row of the course is the address of a run in here: tile numbers from the bottom of the strip up, read as row i at offset i by FreshStrip. Runs overlap: rows one byte apart are the same run shifted by one tile, which is how bends are drawn (docs/06-rendering.md 6.5). The rows of all ten courses point between $C640 and $C8DB.
$C8EA w SegmentRows 2 ; Row lists of the course segments
> A course byte b selects the list at $C8EA + 2 x b: row addresses (into TileRuns), ended by a word with a zero high byte. Lists overlap, so a byte can start inside another byte's list. CourseExpand ($D4D9) copies them into RowList (docs/06-rendering.md 6.5). One line here is the slot of one even course byte.
$C9FE b GroundAnchors 8 ; Ground profile anchors of the course segments
> A course byte b selects the list at $C9FE + b / 2, ended by $FF: one ground anchor byte for every 8 bytes of the profile at $6000 (height in bits 0-4, walls in bits 5-7, docs/07-objects.md 7.4). GroundProfile ($D0EF) interpolates between them.
$CA43 s RowList ; The row list of the current course (filled by CourseExpand)
> 176 words, zero in the image, where CourseExpand writes the course's rows (178 for every course, so the last two land on the first two words of RowListTail). $C3F8 points to the next row a fresh strip starts from.
$CBA3 w RowListTail 7 ; Run-out rows after a course, and the end mark $FFFF
> The rows that follow every course: $C643 and $C655 alternating, then $FFFF at $CBC1. With 178 course rows the list holds 191 rows before the mark. The course check ($D49C) looks at the high byte of the row 15 ahead and ends the course at $FF.
$CBC3 b Course7 16 ; Course data of round 7
> A course: the player's type (0 aircraft, 1 ground vehicle), then one segment byte per course piece (indexes into SegmentRows and GroundAnchors), ended by $FF (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.2, docs/06-rendering.md 6.5). The ten courses are stored in the order of rounds 7, 8, 9, 10, 3, 2, 1, 6, 5, 4.
$CC17 b Course8 16 ; Course data of round 8
$CC6C b Course9 16 ; Course data of round 9
$CCC0 b Course10 16 ; Course data of round 10
$CD0F b Course3 16 ; Course data of round 3
$CD5D b Course2 16 ; Course data of round 2
$CDB6 b Course1 16 ; Course data of round 1
$CE0A b Course6 16 ; Course data of round 6
$CE5D b Course5 16 ; Course data of round 5
$CEAB b Course4 16 ; Course data of round 4
$CF04 w CourseTable 2 ; Course records of rounds 1-10, ended by $FFFF
> Ten records of two words: the course data, and the round's 128-byte object block at $F300 + 128 x (round - 1) (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.2, docs/07-objects.md 7.2). $FFFF ends the table: CourseExpand wraps to $CF04 there.
$CF2E w CourseRecord 1 ; The current course record
> The record of the course being played or scrolled into. NextCourse ($D4CF) steps it by 4.
$CF30 w RestartRecord 1 ; The course record a round start uses
> RoundStart ($CF68) copies it into CourseRecord. Set by the game start, by RoundSetup ($D1E1) and by the look-back ($D027).
@module mainloop
$CF32 c GameEntry ; The game's entry: interrupts off
> Entered from $C2B1 after loading. Interrupts stay off from here on: no pass waits for a frame (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.5). Runs on into MainLoop.
$CF32 ; interrupts off for good
$CF33 c MainLoop ; Menu, game start, round start and the pass loop
> The whole game state machine; it never returns (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.1-4.4). $CF33 calls the menu, then starts a game or the demo; RoundStart ($CF68) starts a round or a retry, pre-rolls 32 strips and enters the pass loop PassLoop ($CFAC). A lost vehicle (CheckLost, $CFEF) retries the round, falls back to an earlier one (LookBack, $D017) or ends in GameOver ($D0A5). The pass also reads the abort (U+G) and pause (P) keys (docs/05-controls.md 5.5). GAME OVER and ABORT return to $CF33.
$CF33 ; the menu; returns when a game or the demo starts
$CF36 ; SCORE ($EF49) = round 0's saved SCORE
$CF41 ; round number $C2F7 = 0
$CF45 ; no sound requests
$CF48 ; carried lives $ECE4 = 4
$CF4D ; a game starts with the record of round 1
$CF50 ; the demo?
$CF56 ; random number in HL
$CF5A ; the demo: one of records 3-10, (L AND $1C) + 8 bytes in
$CF65 KeepRestartRecord ; the record a round start uses
$CF68 RoundStart ; a round start or a retry
$CF6E ; clear the ground profile $6000-$62FF
$CF7A ; strips drawn $C3FA = 0
$CF7E ; scroll distance = 0
$CF82 ; 11 records from $C000: free ($8B), then the end of the list
$CF8A FreeRecordsLoop
$CF91 ; $C2F6, the new height worked on by the mover, = 2
$CF97 ; scroll state: page offset 0, half 0
$CFA0 ; the course into RowList
$CFA3 ; pre-roll: 128 scroll steps, 32 strips; the last one calls RoundSetup
$CFA5 PreRollLoop
$CFAC PassLoop ; one pass of the game
$CFB2 ; " DEMO " in the status window
$CFB9 ; the demo: player's speed 0
$CFBF ShotGauge ; the shot gauge, not during the AUTO start
$CFC5 ; count the free ones of the 5 shot slots (bit 7)
$CFCE CountFreeShots
$CFD3 NextShotSlot
$CFD6 ; the gauge text at $C333: five spaces, then a "/" per free slot
$CFDB BlankGauge
$CFE4 GaugeSlashes
$CFE9 PrintGauge
$CFEF CheckLost ; the player's record gone: the vehicle is lost
$CFF6 ; the loss beep
$CFFC ; lives less one, carried to the next round start
$D003 ; lives left: the same round again
$D005 ; no lives: hide the last dot
$D00A ; a short pause
$D00D ; MAX from SCORE
$D010 ; round 1: GAME OVER
$D017 LookBack ; try the round before, down to round 2
$D01B ; the round number one less; RoundSetup counts it up again
$D023 ; its course record
$D032 ; its round table entry: lives at its start, then SCORE
$D038 ; SCORE back to that round's start
$D040 ; its lives less one
$D046 ; 1-4 lives: restart that round
$D04B SkipThatRound
$D050 RetryRound ; MAX from SCORE
$D061 ; SCORE back to the round's start
$D070 ReadInput ; the device, or the AUTO start
$D073 PassWork ; objects moved and hit tested
$D076 ; objects to screen positions
$D079 ; save the strips under the objects, shadow and sprite lists
$D07C ; shadows and sprites into the playfield buffer (docs/06-rendering.md)
$D07F ; the playfield to the screen, then one scroll step
$D082 ; the requested sound effect
$D085 ; clear the sound requests
$D089 ; U ...
$D091 ; ... and G: abort
$D099 ; "ABORT!"
$D09F ; the demo: no GAME OVER box
$D0A5 GameOver ; the GAME OVER box and two beeps
$D0B1 BackToMenu ; one beep, back to the menu
$D0BA PauseKeys ; P held, ENTER not: pause
$D0CC ; pause beep
$D0D2 ; "PAUSE!"
$D0D8 PauseWait ; wait for ENTER
$D0E6 ; clear the status window
@module roundsetup
$D0EF c BuildGroundProfile ; Build the ground profile $6000 from the course
> Clears $6000-$62FF and fills it with the ground profile of the course (CourseRecord): one byte for every 2 units along, height in bits 0-4 and walls in bits 5-7 (docs/07-objects.md 7.4). Each course byte b after the type gives the anchors at $C9FE + b / 2 (GroundAnchors), ended by $FF; an anchor goes every 8 bytes from $6008, and the 7 bytes after the previous anchor step from it toward the new one by (old height - new height) / 8 a byte. The previous anchor also takes the new anchor's wall bits. Stops when the buffer is full ($6300) or the course ends. Entered by JP from the course check ($D4C2) right after RoundSetup, so it returns to the caller of ScrollStep.
$D0EF ; clear the 768-byte profile
$D0FB ; BC = the course data
$D101 ; skip the player's type
$D102 ; the first anchor; $6000 is the anchor before it
$D105 ProfileSegment ; the next course byte; $FF ends the course
$D10A ; its anchors at $C9FE + b / 2
$D113 ProfileAnchor ; the buffer full at $6300: done
$D118 ; drop the saved course pointer
$D11A NextAnchor ; the next anchor; $FF ends the segment
$D11F ; the new anchor
$D122 ; back to the previous anchor
$D12B ; the previous anchor takes the new walls
$D12F ; (old height - new height) / 8: the step
$D13B ; the 7 bytes in between
$D13D InBetweenLoop
$D142 ; on to the new anchor
$D149 SegmentDone ; the next course byte
$D14D w ScrollDistance 1 ; The distance scrolled into the round
> + 2 a pass (ScrollStep). The positions of the object records along the course are in the same unit (docs/07-objects.md 7.1). Cleared at a round start and by RoundSetup.
$D14F w ConvoyShift 1 ; The player's old position less $6C
> Set by RoundSetup ($D209): subtracted from the positions of the records $C060-$C090 (the followers), so they keep their place behind the player when the positions start again.
$D151 c RoundSetup ; Set up a round: round table, round number, lives, objects, colour
> Called by the course check ($D4BF) when the 32nd strip of a course is drawn: at the end of a round start's pre-roll, and when the next course scrolls in without a break (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.2, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.2). Outside the demo: MAX from SCORE, the round table entry of the round just ended (or retried) gets the carried lives and SCORE, the round number counts up (after 10 back to 1, with the lap $C2F8 counted and round 10's entry copied to round 0's), the round number and the lives are shown. Then, demo or not ($D1DE): the round's objects from the course record (docs/07-objects.md 7.2, 7.8), the round sound, and the course colour painted by falling into PaintAttributes with B = ink 6 - ((round - 1) AND 3), bright below 5; in the demo the subtracted value is one less (the round number stays 0 there, so ink 4, bright green).
$D151 ; the demo skips the score and the panel
$D158 ; MAX from SCORE
$D15B ; round table entry $EF4F + 6 x round
$D169 ; the carried lives
$D16E ; then SCORE, 5 characters
$D177 ; print SCORE
$D17A ; the round's two digits go to $C2FD-$C2FE
$D17D ; the next round
$D181 ; after round 10:
$D185 ; the next lap
$D18C ; round 10's entry into round 0's
$D19B StoreRoundNumber
$D19E ; tens digit: the lap, plus 1 from round 10
$D1AE RoundUnits
$D1B0 ; a leading 0 becomes a space
$D1B6 StoreTensDigit
$D1BC ; print the round number
$D1C2 ; lives = the carried lives
$D1C8 ; hide the 4 dots
$D1CD HideDotsLoop
$D1D2 ; a short pause; A still holds the lives
$D1D9 LifeDotsLoop ; a cyan dot per life
$D1DE ObjectSetup ; the round's objects
$D1E1 ; a retry restarts this course
$D1E4 ; the speed reserve $E7B8 = 10
$D1E9 ; no followers
$D1F3 ; the follower list $ECE9 (pointer $ECF1): empty
$D1FB ClearLinksLoop
$D200 ; how far the player has come, less $6C
$D20C ; the player's start fields +1-+7 from $C292
$D217 ; the scroll distance starts again (BC = 0 after LDIR)
$D21B ; the type of the round before: kept for the record $C260
$D223 ; the parked vehicle $C0A1-$C0AF from $C2A2
$D22B ; the course data
$D232 ; the player's type, the course's first byte
$D236 ; $C2BD = type + 6
$D23B ; the parked vehicle is of the other type
$D242 ; $C008 = 2 x type
$D249 ; AUTO countdown: 70 passes, or 14 for a nonzero type
$D250 SetAutoCountdown
$D254 ; the object block of the round
$D25B ; 16 course objects at $C0B0: 8 bytes from the block, 8 zero
$D25D CourseObjectLoop
$D266 ZeroTailLoop
$D26E ; the 11 slots $C1B0-$C250 free
$D273 FreeSixSlotsLoop
$D27A FreeFiveSlotsLoop
$D27F ; $C260: the vehicle of the round before, at $006C
$D28A ; its fields +3 on from $C2A4
$D294 ; the end of the list ($C270)
$D296 ; the lap: 0 skips the arming
$D29F ; from lap 2, own operands: CP $05 becomes CP $02, types 2-9 fire
$D2A4 ; every launch pad's behaviour becomes $88
$D2AC LapArming ; arm the 16 course objects
$D2B1 ArmObjectLoop ; the type
$D2B8 ; C = the behaviour
$D2BD ; operand changed at $D2A1
$D2C1 ; types 5-9 (2-9 from lap 2): fire
$D2C6 NotFiringType ; type $0E, a launch pad: rotate what it launches
$D2D3 ; others: $88
$D2D7 Pad18To48 ; $18 becomes $48 (operand changed at $D2A6)
$D2DB Pad48To08 ; $48 becomes $08 (operand changed at $D2A9)
$D2DD NextArmedObject
$D2E3 SetField8 ; every record from $C060 to the end: +8 = 2 x type
$D2E9 Field8Loop
$D2F6 ShiftFollowers ; the 4 records $C060-$C090 (the followers)
$D2FC ShiftFollowerLoop ; position along less ConvoyShift
$D30F ; speed 0
$D31A ; the round sound: entry 5 of the effect table $F2E2
$D320 ; the course colour from the round
$D327 ; the demo subtracts one less
$D32E CourseInk ; ink 6 - that
$D331 ; bright below 5
$D337 CourseColourReady
$D338 c PaintAttributes ; Colour the cells of the blit address list with B
> Walks the address list at $D5B2 (BlitAddresses), screen addresses of the bottom lines of character cells ended by a high byte $FF, and sets the attribute of each cell to B. A high byte below $47 (the $0800 clip entries) is skipped; $47 gives row block $58xx, $48-$4F $59xx, $50 and up $5Axx (docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.5). Reached by falling through from RoundSetup with the course colour, and called by the menu with B = 6.
$D33B PaintCell ; the next screen address
$D340 ; attribute row block $58
$D342 ; $FF ends the list
$D345 ; below $47: a clipped entry
$D349 ; $47: block $58
$D34B ; $48-$4F: block $59; $50 and up: $5A
$D350 AttrBlock59
$D351 PaintThisCell ; the cell's colour
$D354 c ShortPause ; A busy wait of 65 536 DJNZ steps
$D356 PauseOuterLoop
$D358 PauseInnerLoop
> About 0.85 million T-states, a quarter of a second. Keeps A. Used after hiding the dots (RoundSetup $D1D2, and on the last life lost at $D00A).
@module scroller
$D35E c Blit ; Copy the 32 strips of the playfield to the screen
> SP walks the address list BlitAddresses ($D5B2): every POP is the screen address of a cell's bottom line, and up to 8 lines are written upward from it (LD A,(DE); LD (HL),A; INC E; DEC H). DE starts at page $80 + $C32E, byte $C32F (the half), and moves 2 pages a column, wrapping in $80-$BF. Even columns take 17 POPs, the first and last writing 4 lines; odd columns 16 POPs of 8 lines. Clip entries point at $0800 in the ROM. Then the sprite lists ($DD6C), and falls into ScrollStep (docs/06-rendering.md 6.3).
$D35E ; SP kept in $C331
$D362 ; SP walks the address list
$D365 ; DE = page $80 + $C32E, byte $C32F
$D376 ; 16 column pairs
$D378 BlitEvenColumn ; an even column: the first cell, 4 lines
$D388 ; 15 full cells
$D38A BlitCellLoop
$D3AC ; the last cell, 4 lines
$D3BB ; 2 pages on, wrapping in $80-$BF
$D3BF ; the same half
$D3C3 ; an odd column: 16 full cells
$D3C5 BlitOddColumn ; one cell, 8 lines up
$D3E7 ; 2 pages on, wrapping in $80-$BF
$D3EF ; the next column pair
$D3F2 ; SP back
$D3F6 ; the sprite lists; then on into the scroll step
$D3F9 c ScrollStep ; One scroll step: toggle the half, make one strip
> Called 128 times by the round start's pre-roll and entered once a pass from Blit. The scroll distance $D14D grows by 2, the half $C32F toggles and the page offset $C32E (0-63) counts up when the half goes back to 0. Each of the four states makes one strip (docs/06-rendering.md 6.4): half 0, offset even: FreshStrip ($D51D); half $80, even: StripPhase4 ($D413); half 0, odd: StripPhase2 ($D45B); half $80, odd: the course check ($D49C).
$D3F9 ; the scroll distance + 2
$D401 ; half $80:
$D407 ; back to half 0
$D40E ; odd offset: the course check
$D44E EnterHalf80 ; half 0: to half $80
$D456 ; even offset: a fresh strip
$D413 c StripPhase4 ; Make the 4-pixel phase in page H - 1, half 0
> H is the page of the last fresh strip ($C3FC). Steps the page offset $C32E. Copies H's half 0 two lines up into page H - 1, half 0 (the two lines below cleared), then shifts it 4 pixels right with RRD, taking the nibble entering from H - 2's half 0 at line 2. The last byte is 0 (docs/06-rendering.md 6.4).
$D413 ; the page offset + 1, mod 64
$D41C ; H = the page of the last fresh strip
$D41F ; DE = page H - 1, half 0, wrapping in $80-$BF
$D429 ; lines 0 and 1 clear
$D42E ; H's half 0, two lines up
$D433 ; HL = page H - 1, half 0
$D434 ; DE = page H - 2, line 2
$D440 ShiftRight4Loop ; 4 pixels right, the nibble from H - 2 coming in
$D447 ; the last two lines: nothing comes in
$D45B c StripPhase2 ; Make the 2-pixel phase in page H - 1, half $80
> Copies H's half 0 one line up into page H - 1, half $80 (line 0 cleared), then shifts it 2 pixels right, the 2 bits entering from H - 2's half 0 at line 3. The last bytes shift with nothing coming in (docs/06-rendering.md 6.4).
$D45B ; H = the page of the last fresh strip
$D45E ; DE = page H - 1, half $80
$D469 ; line 0 clear
$D46C ; H's half 0, one line up
$D471 ; HL = page H - 1, half $80
$D472 ; DE = page H - 2, line 3
$D47E ShiftRight2Loop ; 2 pixels right, 2 bits from H - 2 coming in
$D48B ; the last lines: nothing comes in
$D49C CourseCheck ; the page offset + 1, mod 64
$D4A5 ; the high byte of the row 15 ahead
$D4AD ; $FF: the course has run out
$D4B1 ; strips drawn this course + 1
$D4BB ; the 32nd strip:
$D4BF ; set up the round
$D4C2 ; build the ground profile; returns to ScrollStep's caller
$D4C5 CourseEnd ; the end of a course: the demo?
$D4CB ; the demo: drop the return address, back to the menu
$D4CF NextCourse ; otherwise the next course record, expanded
$D4D9 c CourseExpand ; Expand a course record into RowList
> HL = a course record (CourseRecords). At $FFFF wraps to $CF04 and sets CourseRecord. Each course byte b after the type selects the row list $C8EA + 2 x b (SegmentRows), copied word by word into RowList $CA43 until a word with a zero high byte. Then the next row $C3F8 = $CA43 and the strip count $C3FA = 0 (docs/06-rendering.md 6.5).
$D4D9 ; DE = the course data
$D4DC ; $FFFF ends the table:
$D4E0 ; back to the record of round 1
$D4E9 CourseDataFound
$D4EA ; skip the player's type
$D4EB ; BC = RowList
$D4EE CourseSegment ; the next course byte; $FF ends the course
$D4F4 ; its rows at $C8EA + 2 x b
$D4FC CopyRow ; the next row
$D500 ; a zero high byte ends the list
$D504 ; the row into RowList
$D50C NextCourseByte
$D510 CourseExpanded ; the next row to draw: the first
$D516 ; no strips drawn yet
$D51D c FreshStrip ; Make a fresh strip from tiles, and the phase 2 pixels left of it
> Half 0, even offset. The new strip is page $80 + $C32E, half 0: 8 lines of the edge tile (EdgeTiles), then 15 tiles, tile i read from row i at offset i, rows popped from $C3F8 on; $C3F8 moves one row on. The page is kept in $C3FC. The tail makes page H - 2, half $80: H - 2's half 0 at line 1 shifted 2 pixels left, the 2 bits entering from H copied in at line 3 (docs/06-rendering.md 6.4, 6.5).
$D51D ; the new strip: page $80 + $C32E, half 0
$D527 ; the edge tile 23
$D52A ; ...or 24 for one strip in four
$D536 EdgeLines ; 8 lines of edge
$D546 ; SP kept in $C331
$D54A ; SP walks the rows from $C3F8
$D54E ; the next strip starts one row on
$D553 ; tile i = 0
$D554 FreshTile ; row i at offset i
$D55A ; the tile number
$D55B ; HL = $C400 + 8 x tile
$D564 ; 8 lines of the tile
$D574 ; the next tile, 15 in all
$D57A ; SP back
$D57E ; the page of the fresh strip
$D582 ; DE = page H - 2, half $80
$D58E ; lines 0-2 clear
$D595 ; H's half 0, three lines up
$D59A ; HL = page H - 2, half $80
$D59B ; DE = page H - 2, line 1
$D5A2 ShiftLeft2Loop ; 2 pixels left, 2 bits from H coming in
$D5AF ; the last line clear
$D5B2 w BlitAddresses 9 ; Blit addresses, column 0 (17 cells)
> 528 screen addresses, the bottom line of a character cell each, popped by Blit: 17 for each even column, 16 for each odd one, from the bottom of the screen up. Even columns sit 4 lines off the odd ones; the list carries the 2:1 isometric skew and the clipping, 43 entries $0800 pointing into the ROM (docs/06-rendering.md 6.3). PaintAttributes uses the same list to colour the playfield's cells. Split here by column.
$D5D4 w BlitColumn01 8 ; Blit addresses, column 1 (16 cells)
$D5F4 w BlitColumn02 9 ; Blit addresses, column 2 (17 cells)
$D616 w BlitColumn03 8 ; Blit addresses, column 3 (16 cells)
$D636 w BlitColumn04 9 ; Blit addresses, column 4 (17 cells)
