@module sprites
$D9D6 c BuildSpriteLists ; Shadow and sprite lists of the visible objects, the strips under them saved
> Step 7 of the pass (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.3), after $EFA5 has put the visible records in the list at $6500 (4-byte entries: depth, column, record address; bit 6 of the first byte ends it). Pads and dishes (types 14-15) are not in it; $EA38 draws them first from their own list at $C99E.
> The list is walked twice. The first walk (ShadowListLoop) makes the shadow list at $FE64, each object placed at the height of the ground under it; the second (SpriteListBuild) makes the sprite list at $FF00, each object at its own height (+4). For every entry the playfield strips it will cover are first copied to the save area at $F800, so that $DD6C can put the course back after the blit. The objects are drawn into the playfield buffer $8000-$BFFF, not onto the screen: shadows and sprites by $DDE7 (step 8), before the blit $D35E copies the buffer to the screen (docs/06-rendering.md 6.3, 6.6).
$D9D6 ; pads and dishes (list $C99E)
$D9D9 ; the save area starts again at $F800
$D9DF ; the sprite list $FF00, empty
$D9E7 ; the shadow list $FE64, empty
$D9EF ; the visible records, from $EFA5
$D9F3 c ShadowListLoop ; Shadow list: the next visible record
> One entry of $6500 a time: IX = the record. The column (+B) decides how many strips show: an object 4 columns or more inside the window has all its strips (4 for types 0-3, 3 for 4-10, 2 for 11-13); at either edge the strips that fall outside are dropped, and on the left the start moves on by whole strips (DE + $FC a strip: the next column, 4 lines lower in the buffer) while C counts the skipped mask bytes. Phase (+9) 0 or 2 drops one more strip. The line in the strip is +A plus the ground height from the profile at $6000 (bits 0-4). The entry is finished at ShadowListAdd.
$D9F4 ; bit 6: the end of the list
$D9FB ; end the shadow list, go on with the sprites
$DA00 ShadowEntryRecord ; IX = the record
$DA0B ; type; a dying record (bit 5 set) fails every compare and gets no shadow
$DA1C ; types 14 and up: not here
$DA1E ShadowBig ; types 0-3: 4 strips of 17 lines
$DA29 ; the profile under the record: $5FF6 + position / 2
$DA34 ; entry +2, +3: the profile address
$DA38 ; +A: the line in the strip, +B: the column
$DA3F ; the ground height
$DA48 ; column - 4 = the screen column
$DA4A ; 0-28: every strip shows
$DA53 BigShadowCol30
$DA5B BigShadowCol31
$DA63 BigShadowLeft3 ; columns -3 to -1: the first strips are off the left edge
$DA6D ; C = the mask bytes skipped (17 a strip)
$DA73 BigShadowLeft2
$DA83 BigShadowLeft1
$DA92 BigShadowPhase ; phase 0: one strip fewer
$DA9C BigShadowLines ; 17 lines a strip
$DAA1 ShadowMid ; types 4-10: 3 strips of 11 lines
$DAAC ; the profile one byte further on
$DAC8 ; phase 2 and 3: one column on, 4 lines lower
$DAD4 MidShadowColumn
$DAE4 MidShadowCol31
$DAEC MidShadowLeft2
$DAFC MidShadowLeft1
$DB0B MidShadowPhase ; phase 2: one strip fewer
$DB16 MidShadowLines ; 11 lines a strip
$DB1B ShadowSmall ; types 11-13: 2 strips of 4 lines
$DB26 ; the profile two bytes further on
$DB40 ; one column on
$DB51 SmallShadowLeft1
$DB60 SmallShadowPhase ; phase 0: one strip fewer
$DB6A SmallShadowLines ; 4 lines a strip
$DB6C c ShadowListAdd ; Finish a shadow entry, save the strips under it
> In: A = lines a strip, A' = type, B = strips, C = the mask bytes skipped, D = column, E = line. The column becomes the playfield page through the scroll state $C32E, as the blit maps columns (docs/06-rendering.md 6.2): page $80 + ($C32E + 2 x (column - 4)) mod 64. The 8-byte entry at ShadowListPtr: +0 type, +1 phase, +2/+3 the profile address, +4 strips, +5 mask offset, +6/+7 the buffer address. Then SaveStrips copies what the shadow will cover.
$DB6D ; the scroll state: page of column 0
$DB75 ; wrap into $80-$BF
$DB7E ; +0 type
$DB80 ; +1 phase
$DB87 ; +4 strips
$DB89 ; +5 mask offset
$DB8B ; +6, +7 the address in the playfield buffer
$DB8F ; the next free entry
$DB94 ; save the strips under the shadow
$DB9A c SpriteListBuild ; Sprite list: the visible records at their own heights
> The second walk of $6500. The line is +A plus the height +4. A dying record (bit 5) is drawn as a type 3 explosion, a line lower at height 25 and two lines lower above. The graphic is the word at $C300 + (+8): +8 is twice the type normally (set at $E2A4) and the explosion counter while dying. The phase (+9) picks one of 4 pre-shifted frames: $A8 bytes for types 0-3 (4 strips of 21 lines, a mask and a pixel byte a line), $66 for 4-10 (3 strips of 17), $34 for 11-13 (2 strips of 13). Clipping is as in ShadowListLoop; a dropped strip on the left moves the graphic on by one strip. The 6-byte entry at SpriteListPtr: +0 type, +1 strips, +2/+3 the buffer address, +4/+5 the graphic; then SaveSpriteStrips. $EAE3 draws the list after the shadows.
$DB9E SpriteListLoop
$DBA6 ; end the sprite list
$DBA9 SpriteEntryRecord
$DBB4 ; +A: the line in the strip, +B: the column
$DBBA ; + the height
$DBC3 ; dying?
$DBCA ; at 25 a line lower, above that two
$DBD4 DrawAsExplosion ; dying: drawn as a type 3, the explosion
$DBD6 SpriteByType
$DBE6 ; types 14 and up: not here
$DBE8 SpriteBig ; types 0-3: 4 strips of 21 lines
$DBE9 ; the graphic: the word at $C300 + (+8)
$DBF2 ; one frame: 4 strips x 21 lines x 2 bytes
$DBF5 ; frame (phase - 1) AND 3
$DBFD BigFrameLoop
$DC01 BigSpriteColumn
$DC0F BigSpriteCol30
$DC17 BigSpriteCol31
$DC1F BigSpriteLeft3
$DC23 ; 3 strips off the left: the graphic 3 strips on
$DC31 BigSpriteLeft2
$DC43 BigSpriteLeft1
$DC54 BigSpritePhase
$DC5E BigSpriteLines ; 21 lines a strip
$DC63 SpriteMid ; types 4-10: 3 strips of 17 lines
$DC6D ; one frame: 3 strips x 17 lines x 2 bytes
$DC72 ; phase 2 and 3: one column on, 4 lines lower
$DC7E MidFrameIndex ; frame (phase + 1) AND 3
$DC83 MidFrameLoop
$DC89 MidSpriteColumn
$DC97 MidSpriteCol31
$DC9F MidSpriteLeft2
$DCB1 MidSpriteLeft1
$DCC2 MidSpritePhase
$DCCD MidSpriteLines ; 17 lines a strip
$DCD1 SpriteSmall ; types 11-13: 2 strips of 13 lines
$DCDC ; one frame: 2 strips x 13 lines x 2 bytes
$DCE7 SmallFrameLoop
$DCEB SmallSpriteColumn
$DCF9 SmallSpriteLeft1
$DD0A SmallSpritePhase
$DD14 SmallSpriteLines ; 13 lines a strip
$DD16 SpriteListAdd ; the page from the column, as at ShadowListAdd
$DD29 ; +0 type
$DD2B ; +1 strips
$DD2D ; +2, +3 the address in the playfield buffer
$DD36 ; +4, +5 the graphic
$DD3E ; save the strips under the sprite
$DD44 c SaveSpriteStrips ; Save the strips under the sprite just listed
> HL = the buffer address kept at $D9D4 (HL held the graphic), then SaveStrips.
$DD47 c SaveStrips ; Copy the strips an object will cover into the save area
> In: HL = the playfield address of the first strip's lowest line, B = strips, A = lines a strip. Each strip's lines are copied with LDIR to SaveAreaPtr ($F800 on); the next strip is the next column of the buffer: 4 bytes lower (the 4-line skew of docs/06-rendering.md 6.3), two pages on, wrapping from $C0 to $80. RestoreStrips undoes it after the blit.
$DD47 ; DE = the free save area
$DD4D SaveStripLoop
$DD4E ; BC = lines
$DD55 ; the next strip: 4 lines lower ...
$DD59 ; ... two pages on, $80-$BF
$DD61 ; the save area's new end
$DD66 w ShadowListPtr ; The shadow list's next free entry ($FE64 on); DrawShadows reuses it to walk the list
$DD68 w SpriteListPtr ; The next free entry of the sprite list ($FF00 on)
$DD6A w SaveAreaPtr ; The free end of the strip save area ($F800 on)
$DD6C c RestoreUnderSprites ; After the blit: the strips under every shadow and sprite put back
> Called by the blit $D35E once the buffer is on the screen (docs/06-rendering.md 6.1). Walks the shadow list $FE64 (17, 11 or 4 lines a strip by type) and then the sprite list $FF00 (21, 17 or 13), in the order BuildSpriteLists saved them, and copies the saved bytes back from $F800 with RestoreStrips. The playfield is clean again for the scroller.
$DD6C ; the saved strips, in the order they were saved
$DD72 ; the shadow list
$DD76 RestoreShadowLoop
$DD80 ; +4 strips
$DD83 ; +6, +7 the buffer address
$DD94 RestoreMidShadow
$DD98 RestoreBigShadow ; types 0-3: 17 lines
$DD9A RestoreShadowStrips
$DD9F RestoreSprites ; the sprite list
$DDA3 RestoreSpriteLoop
$DDA9 ; +1 strips
$DDAB ; +2, +3 the buffer address
$DDBE RestoreMidSprite
$DDC2 RestoreBigSprite ; types 0-3: 21 lines
$DDC4 RestoreSpriteStrips
$DDC9 c RestoreStrips ; Copy saved strips back into the playfield buffer
> In: DE = the playfield address of the first strip's lowest line, B = strips, A = lines a strip; the bytes come from SaveAreaPtr on. The reverse of SaveStrips.
$DDC9 ; HL = the next saved bytes
$DDCE RestoreStripLoop
$DDD6 ; the next strip: 4 lines lower, two pages on
@module shadows
$DDE6 b ShadowGround ; The ground height under the shadow being drawn
$DDE7 c DrawShadows ; Shadows into the playfield buffer, then the sprites
> Step 8 of the pass (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.3), before the blit. For every entry of the shadow list $FE64 it ANDs a shadow into the strips at the ground under the object, then jumps to $EAE3, which draws the sprite list $FF00 over it.
> Types 0-3 and 4-10: the shadow is first built as a mask in $6500 (68 bytes, 4 strips of 17, or 33 bytes, 3 strips of 11; set to $FF = clear), from the shape tables below the image ($5CE0, $5DE8, $5EF0 for types 0-3; $5B60, $5BE0, $5C60 for 4-10), at an offset by the phase (+1). The Mask* helpers AND runs of shape bytes into it, strip by strip; each MaskStep* helper moves one profile byte (2 units along) further and shifts its run by the change of the ground height from ShadowGround, so the shadow follows the slope. The mask is then ANDed into the buffer from the entry's mask offset (+5), +4 strips, at the address +6/+7.
> Types 11-13 (ShadowTiny): 4 bytes a strip from $5B00, $5B20 or $5B40 (the ground level, rising by 1, or more over the next profile byte), 8 bytes a phase, ANDed straight into the buffer.
$DDED ShadowsLoop
$DDF3 ; the shadow list is done: the sprites
$DDF7 ; E = +1 phase
$DDF9 ; BC = +2, +3 the profile under the object
$DE08 ShadowBigMask ; types 0-3
$DE09 ; the ground height where the shadow starts
$DE0F ; the mask: 4 strips of 17 bytes, clear
$DE16 ClearBigMaskLoop
$DE1C ; the shape: $5DE8 type 0, $5EF0 type 2, $5CE0 types 1 and 3
$DE2C BigShapeChosen ; one sequence of runs a phase
$DE3C ; phase 0
$DE5E BigMaskPhase1 ; phase 1
$DE83 BigMaskPhase2 ; phase 2
$DEA8 BigMaskPhase3 ; phase 3
$DECB ShadowBigApply
$DED0 ; C = +4 strips
$DED2 ; A = +5 the first mask byte
$DED4 ; DE = +6, +7 the buffer address
$DEE3 BigApplyStripLoop
$DEE6 BigApplyLineLoop ; 17 lines: buffer AND mask
$DF04 ; the next strip: 4 lines lower, two pages on
$DF0F ShadowMidMask ; types 4-10
$DF11 ; the ground height where the shadow starts
$DF16 ; the mask: 3 strips of 11 bytes, clear
$DF1D ClearMidMaskLoop
$DF22 ; the shape: $5B60 type 10, $5C60 types 8-9, $5BE0 types 4-7
$DF34 MidShapeChosen
$DF5F MidMaskPhase1
$DF7A MidMaskPhase2
$DF91 MidMaskPhase3
$DFAA ShadowMidApply
$DFC2 MidApplyStripLoop ; 11 lines: buffer AND mask
$E008 ShadowTiny ; types 11-13
$E00C ; the ground rise to the next profile byte
$E011 ; flat: $5B00
$E017 ; a rise of 1: $5B20
$E01E ; any other change, a fall too: $5B40
$E021 TinyShapeChosen ; + 8 x phase
$E02B ; + the skipped bytes (+5)
$E03A TinyStripLoop
$E03B ; 4 lines a strip: buffer AND shape
$E05C c MaskStepRuns5x3 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns5x3
> The MaskStep* entries share one head: BC moves to the next profile byte; E = $6500 + D + (its ground height - ShadowGround), the mask position of the run in this strip, raised by the slope. They fall into the matching MaskRuns* entry.
$E05C ; the ground height where the shadow starts
$E060 ; the next profile byte
$E064 ; its ground, relative
$E065 ; + the run's place in the mask
$E06B c MaskRuns5x3 ; AND 5 shape bytes into the mask at DE, then 3 more 7 on
> In: HL = the shape, DE = the mask in $6500. The MaskRuns* entries AND short runs of shape bytes into the mask: a run, a gap, a run (the gap is the step to the next strip of the mask), HL running on. This one: 5, a gap of 7, 3.
$E075 c MaskStepRuns4x4 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns4x4
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E084 c MaskRuns4x4 ; AND 4 shape bytes, then 4 more 7 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 4 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 4 more. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E08E c MaskStepRuns3x5 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns3x5
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E09D c MaskRuns3x5 ; AND 3 shape bytes, then 5 more 7 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 3 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 5 more. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E0A7 c MaskStepRuns2x6 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns2x6
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E0B6 c MaskRuns2x6 ; AND 2 shape bytes, then 6 more 7 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 2 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 6 more. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E0C6 c MaskStepRuns5x5 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns5x5
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E0D5 c MaskRuns5x5 ; AND 5 shape bytes, then 5 more 11 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 5 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 5 more. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E0DF c MaskStepRuns4x6 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns4x6
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E0EE c MaskRuns4x6 ; AND 4 shape bytes, then 6 more 11 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 4 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 6 more. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E0F8 c MaskStepRuns3x7x3 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns3x7x3
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E107 c MaskRuns3x7x3 ; AND 3 shape bytes, 7 more 11 on, 3 more 11 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 3 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 7, then 3. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E118 c MaskStepRuns1x6x4 ; Next profile byte, then MaskRuns1x6x4
> DrawShadows calls it between two runs of a big or mid shadow shape: BC on to the next profile byte (the ground under the next column), DE = SpriteScratch + (that byte AND $1F) - ShadowGround + D, so the next runs of the shape are ANDed into the mask where the ground there puts them; then it falls into the runs entry below.
$E127 c MaskRuns1x6x4 ; AND 1 shape byte, 6 more 12 on, 4 more 11 on
> The mask shapes are runs of shape bytes ANDed into the mask at $6500 (SpriteScratch) through the chain MaskAnd7-MaskAnd3: 1 bytes, then E + 7 (the mask is strips of 17 bytes), then 6, then 4. Used by DrawShadows; docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$E139 c MaskAnd7 ; AND 7 shape bytes into the mask
> In: HL = the shape, DE = the mask. One chain of entries: MaskAnd7, MaskAnd6, MaskAnd5, MaskAnd4 and MaskAnd3 AND that many bytes (HL and E move on; E stays on the last byte).
$E13E c MaskAnd6 ; AND 6 shape bytes into the mask
> Entry points of one chain: MaskAnd6, MaskAnd5, MaskAnd4, MaskAnd3 AND 6, 5, 4 or 3 shape bytes (HL) into the shadow mask (DE), each falling into the next; see MaskAnd7.
$E143 c MaskAnd5 ; AND 5 shape bytes into the mask
> An entry into the AND chain (MaskAnd6 above): AND the remaining shape bytes into the shadow mask.
$E148 c MaskAnd4 ; AND 4 shape bytes into the mask
> An entry into the AND chain (MaskAnd6 above): AND the remaining shape bytes into the shadow mask.
$E14D c MaskAnd3 ; AND 3 shape bytes into the mask
> An entry into the AND chain (MaskAnd6 above): AND the remaining shape bytes into the shadow mask.
@module mover
$E15C c MoveObjects ; Objects: move, steer, height, walls and hits, once a pass
> Step 5 of the pass (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.3, docs/07-objects.md 7.4). Counts the passes in $C271, then moves every live record of $C000 with MoveRecord. The new position along goes to $C2EA, across to $C2F2, the height to $C2F6 (for the player, $C2F6 already holds the height $E7E9 or AUTO chose); StoreMove writes them back unless the record stopped.
$E15C ; the pass counter: steering every 4 passes, height every 2
$E167 ; the player gone: start with the next record
$E16E c MoveRecord ; Move one record
> IX = the record. In order: the distance ahead of the scroll ($D14D): below 0 the record is gone, 352 or more it waits. A dying record (bit 5) counts its explosion. With behaviour bit 3, at 190 or more ahead, every 8th pass (+F) and on a random bit, it fires: pads and dishes (types 14-15) launch the template $C2B4 + 9k (k from the highest of behaviour bits 7-4) into a free slot of $C1B0-$C200; the others fire the template $C2E1 into the first free of the 6 slots from $C210 (which takes in $C260). A record with no speed and no height behaviour stops here, except the player.
> Then the step (direction table $C340 times the speed +6), steering by behaviour bits 4-7, the height by bits 0-2, the terrain (TerrainHeight), walls and the barrier from the profile, and the hit boxes for PairScan (docs/07-objects.md 7.4, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.3, 10.4).
$E174 ; minus the distance scrolled
$E17C ; behind the scroll: gone
$E187 AheadOfScroll ; $160 (352) or more ahead: not yet
$E194 WithinRange ; dying?
$E19B ; behaviour bit 3: fires
$E1A1 ; less than 190 ahead: no
$E1AB ; the fire timer: every 8th pass
$E1B6 ; and on a random bit
$E1BD ; an enemy shot: template $C2E1, slots from $C210
$E1C8 ; pads and dishes launch instead
$E1CC ; the highest of behaviour bits 7-4 picks the template
