$E93A ; the followers' +6 get the same speed
$E93F FollowerSpeedLoop
$E94C NextFollowerSpeed
$E94F WallCheck ; the profile under the player: $6000 - 9 + position / 2
$E95E ; C = 0 if the first byte is a bit 7 wall, 1 if bit 6, 2 if neither
$E969 OrProfileBytes ; six bytes ORed
$E96A OrProfileLoop
$E96E ; no wall: the track's full width
$E973 ; bit 7 wall: limit $17
$E976 ; already beside that wall: only limited
$E979 WallCrash ; past the limit: the player crashes into the wall
$E982 ; hit
$E988 ; the explosion counter
$E98D ; back 2 along
$E997 ; sound request bit 1
$E99C ; the convoy is lost
$E9A0 ; every follower in the links is hit
$E9A5 FollowerCrashLoop
$E9BD NextFollowerCrash
$E9C4 CheckWall27 ; bit 6 wall: limit $27
$E9CE SteerAcross ; left and right
$E9D3 ; left: the counter $E689 grows to 6
$E9DF StoreLeftCount
$E9E2 ; step 2 * ((counter + 1) / 2)
$E9E7 ; left raises the position across, below the limit E
$E9F0 StoreLeftAcross
$E9F4 ; right: the counter $E68A
$EA00 StoreRightCount
$EA08 ; right lowers it, not below 2
$EA13 AcrossFloor
$EA15 StoreRightAcross
$EA19 c Random ; The random number generator
$EA32 StoreSeed
> Returns a new 16-bit value in HL and keeps it in the seed $EA36: about 254 * (seed + 1) mod 65537, done with three subtractions instead of a multiply (checked by simulating the code over all seeds: 126 of 65 536 differ). The menu bumps the seed every pass of its loop ($F20B), so the player's time in the menu picks the sequence. Called by the game start (the demo's course, $CF56) and the mover ($E16E). docs/05-controls.md 5.1.
$EA36 w RandomSeed ; The random seed
> The state of $EA19, incremented by every pass of the menu loop.

@module statics
$EA38 c StampStatics ; Static objects into the playfield buffer
> Called first by the sprite pass $D9D6. Walks the list at $C99E (record addresses, $FF ends it) that $EFDC builds of the visible records of types 14 and 15, the launch pads and the dishes. A record is drawn only while its column +B is $20-$23, the strips at the far edge of the buffer: 4, 3, 2 or 1 strips of a masked sprite (14 mask and data byte pairs a strip; at column $20 with phase 0, 3 strips). The target is in the playfield buffer $8000-$BFFF (high byte from the scroll offset $C32E + 2 * column + $78), the sprite from the pointer table at $C300 + (+8), stepped $70 bytes times ((phase +9) - 1) AND 3. So these objects are stamped into the course picture as it scrolls in, not drawn per pass. docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$EA38 ; the list of types 14-15, made by $EFDC
$EA3C NextStaticRecord
$EA43 ; $FF: the end
$EA49 ; +A plus the height: the line in the strip
$EA56 ; only columns $20-$23, the edge being drawn
$EA64 NotColumn21
$EA6C NotColumn22
$EA74 FirstEdgeColumn ; column $20: 4 strips, 3 with phase 0
$EA7E StaticTarget ; the buffer row of the column
$EA8C ; the sprite: pointer table at $C300 + (+8)
$EA95 ; + $70 times ((phase - 1) AND 3): phase 1 first, phase 0 last
$EAA0 PhaseStepLoop
$EAA4 StaticFrameReady
$EAA6 StaticStripLoop ; one strip: 14 bytes masked in (AND mask, OR data)
$EAA9 StaticByteLoop
$EAD9 ; the next strip: 2 rows of the buffer on, wrapping in $8000-$BFFF
$EAE3 c DrawSprites ; The moving objects into the playfield buffer
> Reached by a jump from $DDE7 after the strips under the sprites were saved. Walks the sprite list at $FF00 that $D9D6 rebuilds each pass: 6-byte entries of kind, strip count, buffer address and sprite address, $FF ends it (the pointer is kept at $DD68). Each strip is masked in (AND mask, OR data): 21 bytes for kinds 0-3, 17 for kinds 4-10, 13 for 11 and up, then 2 rows on in the buffer, wrapping inside $8000-$BFFF. docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$EAE3 ; the sprite list
$EAE9 NextSpriteEntry
$EAEC ; kind; $FF ends the list
$EAF1 ; strip count, buffer address, sprite address
$EB06 ; the kind picks the height of a strip
$EB0F DrawSpriteTall ; kinds 0-3: 21 bytes a strip
$EB12 TallByteLoop
$EB45 DrawSpriteMid ; kinds 4-10: 17 bytes a strip
$EB48 MidByteLoop
$EB7B DrawSpriteShort ; kinds 11 and up: 13 bytes a strip
$EB7E ShortByteLoop

@module hits
$EBAA c HitDispatch ; A pair hit: jump through the handler table $C380
> Reached from the pair scan $E56F when the other record (HL, on the stack) is within the moving record's box. The same record is skipped ($E57F). Otherwise the moving record's type pair (IX+0 AND $0E) picks the row and the other's the column of the 8 x 8 word table at $C380, and the handler is entered with the other record's address on the stack. Handlers leave by $E57E (pop it and test the next record), $E57F (next, already popped), $E674 (the moving record is done: its new position is not stored, $E65F is skipped) or $E65F via $EBD1. docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$EBAA ; the other record
$EBB0 ; the record against itself: skip it
$EBB6 NotItself
$EBB7 ; column: the other's type pair * 2
$EBBC ; row: the moving record's type pair * 16
$EBC4 ; $C380 + row + column
$EBCD ; the handler, the other record still on the stack
$EBCE c HitScore ; Points, then both hit
> B tens of points ($EE87), then $EBD1. Entered with B = 21 (dome type 2), 32 (dome type 3, after it released a pod), 14 (a missile or a jet) or 18 (a cube on its 4th hit). docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.7.
$EBD1 c HitBoth ; Both records are hit
> Both records get bit 5 (hit) and the explosion counter +8 = $20; a moving one is slowed to speed 1. Sound request bit 1. Ends by storing the moving record's new position ($E65F). The table's cells for the player against the others (row 0, columns 0-4) and the others against the player (column 0) lead here. docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$EBD1 ; the other record
$EBD8 ; a moving one slows to speed 1 while it explodes
$EBDE OtherExplodes ; +8: the explosion counter
$EBE2 MovingHit ; the same for the moving record (entered here by $EBFC and $EC01)
$EBF0 MovingExplodes
$EBF4 ; sound request bit 1: an explosion
$EBFC c HitShotOnShot ; Handler: a shot against a shot (not run in the recordings)
> Reached through the table only (row 6, column 6). POP HL; SET 7,(HL); JR $EBE2. The other shot is removed and the moving one explodes as in $EBD1, sound bit 1.
$EC01 c HitPlayerOnStatic ; Handler: the player against a pad or a dish (not run)
> Row 0, column 7: POP HL; JR $EBE2. Only the player is hit (and explodes, sound bit 1); the pad or dish stays.
$EC04 c HitBlockOnOther ; Handler: a cube or pod against types 2-11 (not run)
> Row 5, columns 1-5: BIT 0,(IX+$00); JP NZ,$E57E; then falls into $EC0B. A pod goes on with the scan, a cube ends it. Nothing is hit.
$EC0B c HitNone ; Handler: nothing happens
> POP HL; JP $E674: the pair has no effect and the moving record's scan ends for this pass. The most common handler (609 runs in the first recording). docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$EC0F c HitTurnRound ; Handler: two missiles of types 4-5 turn round
> Both directions + 4 (mod 8). docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$EC27 c HitPlayerOnShot ; Handler: the player against a shot
> The player's own shots fly in direction 0 and are ignored ($E57E); any other shot hits both ($EBD1). docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$EC2D ; the shot's direction: 0 is the player's own
$EC35 c HitShotOnPlayer ; Handler: a shot against the player (not run)
> Row 6, column 0, the mirror of $EC27: LD A,(IX+$05); AND A; JP Z,$E57E; JP $EBD1.
$EC3F c HitTargetOnShot ; Handler: a missile or a jet (types 4-9) against a shot
> A shot of the player (direction 0): 140 points and both hit. An enemy shot: ignored while the missile moves, else the shot is removed. docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.7.
$EC47 ; 14 tens: 140 points
$EC4C ; an enemy shot on a moving missile: no effect
$EC54 ; on a standing one: the shot is removed
$EC59 c HitShotOnTarget ; Handler: a shot against a missile or a jet
> The mirror of $EC3F with the shot moving: a shot of the player scores 140 and both are hit; an enemy shot goes on past a moving target and is removed by a standing one. All five point awards of the first recording came this way. docs/07-objects.md 7.6.
$EC5D ; 14 tens: 140 points
$EC6C ; an enemy shot on a standing target: removed
$EC73 c HitBlockOnShot ; Handler: a cube or a pod against a shot (not run)
$ECA6 RemoveOtherRecord
> Row 5, column 6, the mirror of $ECAC with the cube moving: an enemy shot or a pod ends at $ECA6 (POP HL; SET 7,(HL): the shot is removed); a shot of the player counts a hit in the cube's +F, at the 4th it scores 180 ($EBCE, B = 18), otherwise the shot explodes (+6 = 0, +8 = $20) and sound request bit 2 ($EC9E).
$ECAC c HitShotOnBlock ; Handler: a shot against a cube or a pod
> An enemy shot, or any shot on a pod, is removed ($ECDC). A shot of the player counts a hit in the cube's +F: the 4th scores 180 points and both are hit; hits 1-3 only explode the shot, with sound request bit 2. docs/07-objects.md 7.6, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.7.
$ECB5 ; the other is a pod: the shot just goes
$ECB9 ; +F: the cube's hits
$ECC2 ; 4th hit: 18 tens, 180 points
$ECC7 ; hits 1-3: the shot explodes, the cube stays
$ECD4 ; sound request bit 2
$ECDC RemoveShot ; the shot is removed
$ECE4 b Followers ; The number of followers (the lives carried into the next round)
> The convoy behind the player, up to 4. A hit on the player costs the last one instead of the vehicle ($E262); the next round's lives are these ($D151 copies them into $C2F9); the game start sets 4 ($CF36-$CF65); the round setup clears it after taking the lives ($D1DE). docs/07-objects.md 7.5, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.4.
$ECE5 w FollowerOffset ; Where the next follower goes, along, from the player
> Added to a new follower's position: -10 at each round setup ($D1DE), then 6 less for each follower ($ED25); a lost follower gives 6 back ($E262).
$ECE7 w ConvoyLinks 5 ; The convoy: the leader's and the followers' +3 addresses
> Five words: $C003 (the player's position across), then the +3 address of each follower in the order they joined; $FF in the high byte marks an empty link (the round setup $D1DE fills the four with $FF, the image holds 0). $ECF3 appends, $E7B9 copies along them, $E979 hits every follower in them.
$ECF1 w ConvoyLinkEnd ; The next free convoy link
$ECF3 c HitPlayerOnPod ; Handler: the player against a cube or a pod
> A cube hits both ($EBD1). A pod (type 11) joins the convoy: it takes the player's +1-+7 (position, across, height, direction, speed, behaviour), is put FollowerOffset behind, its +3 address is appended to the links, the offset grows by 6, $ECE4 counts it, and sound request bit 0 plays. The scan goes on. docs/07-objects.md 7.5.
$ECF5 ; bit 0 clear: a cube (10), both are hit
$ED04 ; the player's +1..+7 into the pod
$ED0A ; along: behind the player by the offset
$ED19 ; its +3 into the next convoy link
$ED25 ; the next one 6 further back
$ED2C ; one more follower
$ED33 ; sound request bit 0: a pod joins
$ED3B c HitBlockOnPlayer ; Handler: a cube or a pod against the player (not run)
> Row 5, column 0, the mirror of $ECF3 with the pod moving: BIT 0,(IX+$00); JP Z,$EBD1, then the same join with IX and HL swapped, ending in sound request bit 0 ($ED79) and JP $E674.
$ED81 c HitDomeOnShot ; Handler: a dome against a shot (not run)
$ED98 DomeHitByPlayer
$EDA9 PodSlotLoop
$EDB3 PodFromDome
> Row 1, column 6, the mirror of $EDCA with the dome moving: an enemy shot passes a moving dome and is removed by a standing one; a shot of the player on a type 2 dome scores 210, on a type 3 dome it releases a pod into a free slot of $C060-$C090 ($EDB3) and scores 320.
$EDCA c HitShotOnDome ; Handler: a shot against a dome
> An enemy shot passes a moving dome and is removed by a standing one ($EDDC). A shot of the player on a dome of type 2: 210 points and both hit. On type 3: a pod (type 11) is written into the first free slot of $C060-$C090 at the dome's place (+1-+6), its +8 = $16, and 320 points; with no free slot, both are just hit. docs/07-objects.md 7.5.
$EDCE ; direction 0: the player's shot
$EDDC ; an enemy shot on a standing dome: removed
$EDE3 ShotHitsDome ; the player's shot
$EDE5 ; type 2: 21 tens, 210 points
$EDEC ; type 3: a free pod slot
$EDF4 PodSlotLoop2
$EDFE ReleasePod ; type 11 at the dome's place
$EE0E ; 32 tens: 320 points

@module hud
$EE13 w PrintScreenPos ; The print routine's screen address
> The bitmap address of the next character cell, kept in memory because the glyph copy needs B and DE. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.1.
$EE15 w PrintAttrPos ; The print routine's attribute address
$EE17 c PrintString ; Print a string of records
$EE18 PrintRecord
> HL = the string: records of attribute + $80, column, row, characters $20-$5F; the next byte with bit 7 set starts a new record, $FF ends the string. Glyphs from the font at $6300, 8 lines each. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.1.
$EE1B ; the attribute, kept in A'
$EE1E ; the screen third by the row: $40, $48 or $50
$EE36 RowInThird ; (row AND 7) * 32 + column
$EE41 ; attribute address: $5800 + row * 32 + column
$EE50 PrintCharLoop ; a character, or bit 7: the next record
$EE56 ; glyph: $6300 + (code - $20) * 8
$EE68 GlyphLineLoop ; 8 lines down the cell
$EE72 ; the attribute
$EE7A ; the next cell
$EE87 c AddPoints ; Add B tens of points to SCORE
> SCORE is ASCII ($EF49-$EF4E, the last digit a fixed '0'): one ten at a time, a space counting as '0', with carry to the left. Falls into $EE9E. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.4.
$EE87 ; the tens digit
$EE8A AddTenToDigit
$EE8B ; a leading space counts as 0
$EE91 DigitUp
$EE96 ; carry into the next digit to the left
$EE9B StoreDigit
$EE9C ; B times
$EE9E c ShowScore ; Draw SCORE in the panel
> SCORE's digits at $529D in small digits ($EEA4). Called by the round setup $D151 too.
$EEA4 c DrawDigits ; Draw a number in small digits, right to left
> DE = the screen address of the units digit, BC = its character. Draws to the left until a '.' byte before the field, each digit from lines 2-6 of its glyph ($6302 + (code - $20) * 8). docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.4.
$EEA5 ; '.': the field starts here
$EEB1 ; lines 2-6 of the glyph
$EEB8 SmallDigitLineLoop
$EEC4 c ClearScores ; Clear SCORE, MAX and the round table
$EEC9 ClearRoundTableLoop
$EED3 ClearScoreLoop
$EEDD ClearMaxLoop
> Spaces into the round table entries of rounds 1-10 ($EF55, 60 bytes), SCORE and MAX; HIGH is kept. Called by the menu $F1AA when it is entered, not after the demo. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.4.
$EEE3 c UpdateHigh ; MAX into HIGH if higher, and draw HIGH
> First updates MAX from SCORE ($EF17). Then compares MAX with HIGH digit by digit as strings (a space sorts below the digits) and copies MAX into HIGH when it is higher. HIGH is drawn at $51FD. Called by the menu $F1AA when it is entered, not after the demo. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.4, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.7.
$EEEE HighCompareLoop ; the first digit that differs decides
$EEFC HighDigitDiffers
$EF02 MaxIntoHigh ; MAX is higher
$EF0E DrawHigh ; HIGH in the panel
$EF17 c UpdateMax ; SCORE into MAX if higher, and draw MAX
$EF1F MaxCompareLoop
$EF2D MaxDigitDiffers
> The same string compare, SCORE against MAX; MAX is drawn at $51BD. Called by the round setup $D151, at every loss ($CF33) and by $EEE3. docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.7.
$EF33 ScoreIntoMax ; SCORE is higher
$EF3F DrawMax ; MAX in the panel
$EF48 b ScoreStop ; '.': the left end of SCORE for $EEA4
$EF49 t Score ; SCORE: five characters and a fixed '0'
> Six ASCII characters; leading spaces stay spaces until a carry reaches them. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.4.
$EF4F b RoundTable 6 ; The round table: 6 bytes a round, rounds 0-10
> Each entry: the lives at the round's start, then SCORE's five characters at that moment. $D151 writes a round's entry at every setup; a lost life or a fall back restores SCORE from it. docs/04-game-loop.md 4.2, 4.4.
$EF91 b HighStop ; '.': the left end of HIGH
$EF92 t HighScore ; HIGH: kept until the machine is reset
$EF98 b MaxStop ; '.': the left end of MAX
$EF99 t MaxScore ; MAX: the best SCORE of this game
$EF9F w DrawListEnd ; The end of the draw list at $6500
> Written by $EFA5 and $F089.
$EFA1 w DepthListEnd ; The end of the depth list at $FF00
$EFA3 w StaticListEnd ; The end of the static-object list at $C99E

@module objview
$EFA5 c PlaceObjects ; Objects to screen places and depth lists
> Once a pass (docs/04-game-loop.md 4.3, step 6). Starts three lists: $C99E (record addresses of visible types 14-15, $FF ends it), $FF00 (4-byte entries sorted by height, a $40 entry ends it) and $6500 (the same, ended by $40). Calls $EFDC for every live record of $C000 and at the end of the list jumps to $F089. docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$EFA9 ; the static-object list, empty
$EFB2 ; the depth list, only the $40 end entry
$EFBD ; the draw list, only the $40 end entry
$EFC8 PlaceRecordsLoop
$EFCB ; bit 6: the end of the records
$EFD0 ; bit 7: gone, not shown
$EFDC c PlaceObject ; One record: its screen place, into a list
> The distance ahead of the scroll (+1,+2 less $D14D) less the position across must be 1-$11F, else the record is not shown. It sets +B = (distance - across + 1) / 8, the column of strips; +9 = ((distance - across) / 2) AND 3, the phase within the strip, 1 while exploding; +A = $C32F + across + $10 + phase. Types 14 and 15 go to the list at $C99E (stamped into the buffer by $EA38); the others are inserted into the $FF00 list by height (+4), as height, (distance + across) / 2 + 1, record address. 41 391 calls in the first recording. docs/06-rendering.md 6.6, docs/07-objects.md 7.1.
$EFE2 ; less the distance scrolled
$EFF1 ; less the position across
$EFF5 ; behind the view: not shown
$EFFE ; $120 or more ahead: not shown
$F002 InViewAhead
$F010 ; +B: the strip column
$F014 ; +9: the phase, 0-3
$F01A ; exploding: phase 1
$F022 StorePhase
$F026 ; +A: the line in the strip
$F035 ; types 14-15: the static list
$F04B DepthListInsert ; (distance + across) / 2 + 1
$F057 ; the first entry higher than this record
$F05A FindHeightSlot
$F064 OpenDepthGap ; open a 4-byte gap: the tail moves up
$F07D ; height, depth, record address
$F089 c SortDrawList ; The depth list into the draw list at $6500
> Each entry of $FF00 (height, depth, record) is inserted into the list at $6500 before the first entry more than 9 above it, or within 9 in height and with a smaller depth byte. So among objects that overlap in height, the farther one comes first. The draw list is read by the sprite pass $D9D6. docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$F08C SortEntryLoop ; bit 6: the $40 end entry
$F099 FindDrawSlot ; heights within 9 of each other?
$F0A2 ; more than 9 higher: insert here
$F0AD CompareDepth ; overlapping: by the depth byte
$F0B8 BackToEntryStart
$F0B9 InsertDrawEntry ; open a 4-byte gap and copy the entry in

@module menu
$F0DB b DemoFlag ; The demo: $FF while the demo plays
> Set by the menu (key 5, or when idle too long), cleared when the menu is entered. docs/05-controls.md 5.1, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.6.
$F0DC b MenuIdleLow ; The menu's idle count, low byte
$F0DD w MenuIdleHigh ; The menu's idle count in 256 passes; 2 000 starts the demo
$F0DF w MenuBarAttr ; The attribute address of the device highlight bar
$F0E1 t DemoMessage ; " DEMO " at (2,6)
> Printed every pass of the demo. Strings are records for $EE17: attribute + $80, column, row, characters; $FF ends. docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.3.
$F0EB t PauseMessage ; "PAUSE!" at (2,6)
$F0F5 t GameOverBox ; The GAME OVER box
> A red frame of $2E blocks around the eleven big-letter glyphs $22-$2C, rows 9-11, columns 10-22. Printed at GAME OVER and ABORT ($D0A5). docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.2-8.3.
$F12C t AbortMessage ; "ABORT!" at (2,6)
$F136 t BlankMessage ; Six spaces at (2,6): clears the message window
$F140 t MenuText ; The menu: SELECT and the six choices
> Seven records: SELECT at (2,6); the four devices at (10,8-11) in white; 5 DEMO and 6 START GAME at (10,13-14) in green. docs/05-controls.md 5.1.
$F1AA c Menu ; The menu: device, demo or start
> Paints the cells of the list $D5B2 with attribute 6 ($D338, docs/08-hud-and-text.md 8.5). Unless it is entered after the demo, it updates HIGH ($EEE3) and clears SCORE, MAX and the round table ($EEC4). Prints the menu and the credits, waits for all keys to be released, beeps. Then it loops: keys 1-4 choose the device ($C28F) and move the 13-cell highlight bar; 5 starts the demo; 6 starts the game; 256 * 2 000 passes with no key start the demo. Every pass increments the random seed $EA36. docs/05-controls.md 5.1.
$F1AC ; attribute 6 over the cells of the list $D5B2
$F1AF ; returning from the demo: the scores are left alone
$F1B5 ; MAX into HIGH
$F1B8 ; a new game's SCORE, MAX and round table
$F1BB MenuKeepScores
$F1BF ; the menu, then the credits stored in the font
$F1D1 MenuReleaseWait ; wait until no key is pressed
$F1D9 ; the menu beep
$F1DF MenuShowDevice ; the idle count starts again
$F1EC ; the bar: attribute row 8 + device, column 10
$F1F5 BarRowLoop
$F1F9 BarRowFound
$F1FE BarHighlightLoop ; 13 cells highlighted
$F203 DeviceReleaseWait ; wait until no key is pressed
$F20B MenuLoop ; the random seed, once a pass
$F212 ; the idle count
$F222 ; 2 000 * 256 passes: the demo
$F22A MenuReadKeys ; key 6: start the game (bar on row 14)
$F235 ; key 5: the demo
$F23D StartDemo ; the demo (bar on row 13)
$F245 HighlightChoice
$F247 ChoiceBarLoop
$F24C ; the start beep
$F253 MenuDevice ; keys 1-4: the device
$F26D DeviceChosen
$F26E ; the chosen device
$F271 ; the device beep
$F277 ; the old bar back to white
$F27C BarWhiteLoop
$F284 c BeepAt ; Beep with the length and pitch at HL
> HL points to a word: the length (high byte) and the pitch (low byte) for $F288. Used by the effect table ($F2D9).
$F288 c Beep ; A two-tone beep
> H = length in 256-step units, L = pitch. Two square waves with half-periods of 256 - L and 257 - L steps (D and E count up to the wrap) toggle the beeper and MIC bits; their beating gives the warble. Border black. docs/09-sound.md 9.6.
$F28E BeepStepLoop ; the beeper, border 0
$F290 ; wave 1: 256 - L steps
$F296 BeepWaveTwo ; wave 2: 257 - L steps
$F29D BeepNextStep
$F2A3 c Sweep ; A rising sweep
$F2AC SweepToneLoop
> HL points to four bytes: the step, the cycles of each tone, the start half-period and the number of tones. Each tone is the step shorter than the one before, so the pitch rises. docs/09-sound.md 9.6.
$F2AE SweepHalfPeriod ; one half-period
$F2B1 SweepWaitLoop
$F2B8 ; the next tone, shorter by the step
$F2BF b SoundRequests ; The sound requests: one bit an effect
> Set by the game (bit 0 a pod joins, 1 an explosion, 2 a hit that does not destroy, 3 AUTO, 4 a shot), played by $F2C0, cleared by the play loop ($D085). docs/09-sound.md 9.6, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.8.
$F2C0 c PlayEffect ; Play the requested effect of the highest priority
> The lowest set bit of $F2BF (bits 0-4) picks the 5-byte entry $F2E2 + 5 * bit; nothing requested, nothing played. docs/09-sound.md 9.6.
$F2C5 LowestBitLoop ; the lowest set bit
$F2CB EffectFound ; entry = 5 * bit
$F2D9 c PlaySound ; Play one effect entry
> HL = a 5-byte entry: 0 and a beep word for $F288, or not 0 and four sweep bytes for $F2A3. The round setup plays the entry $F2FB through here.
$F2E2 b EffectTable 5 ; The five request effects
> Entry per request bit 0-4: kind (0 beep, 1 sweep), then the beep's pitch and length or the sweep's step, cycles, start half-period and tones. Bit 0 sweep 2,4,$50,$1E; bit 1 sweep $0A,3,$14,6; bit 2 sweep $32,5,$1E,3; bit 3 beep $0414; bit 4 sweep 2,4,$42,$0A. docs/09-sound.md 9.6.
$F2FB b RoundStartSound 5 ; The sweep of the round setup
> Sweep 8, 4, 3, $64, played by $D151 through $F2D9 when a round is set up.

@module coursedata
$F300 b RoundObjects1 8 ; The objects of round 1
> 16 records of 8 bytes, copied into the object slots $C0B0-$C1A0 at the round setup: +0 type, +1,+2 position along, +3 across, +4 height, +5 direction, +6 speed, +7 behaviour bits (docs/07-objects.md 7.1-7.4). The course record of a round ($CF04, 4 bytes) points here: round 1 at $F300, then 128 bytes a round. docs/04-game-loop.md 4.2, docs/10-gameplay-logic.md 10.2.
$F380 b RoundObjects2 8 ; The objects of round 2
$F400 b RoundObjects3 8 ; The objects of round 3
$F480 b RoundObjects4 8 ; The objects of round 4
$F500 b RoundObjects5 8 ; The objects of round 5
$F580 b RoundObjects6 8 ; The objects of round 6
$F600 b RoundObjects7 8 ; The objects of round 7
$F680 b RoundObjects8 8 ; The objects of round 8
$F700 b RoundObjects9 8 ; The objects of round 9
$F780 b RoundObjects10 8 ; The objects of round 10
$F800 b SpriteSaveBuffer 16 ; The strips under the sprites, saved
> $D9D6 starts the pointer $DD6A at $F800 each pass and $DD47 copies the buffer strips under each sprite here before the sprites are drawn; $DDC9 puts them back. Nothing in the image here is read before it is written: a fill pattern ($FA,$FC) and, toward the end, bytes that look like Z80 code that never ran. docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$FDFD c Im2Jump ; The IM 2 jump
> Every interrupt vector of the table $FE00-$FF00 is $FDFD. The start-up $9F4B writes JP $9F68 here (EI; RET) and the title player $803A writes JP $8340, its handler; the image holds the latter. The game proper runs with interrupts off. docs/02-architecture.md 2.3-2.4.
$FE00 b Im2Table 16 ; The IM 2 vector table, then the shadow list
> During the title, $9F4B fills $FE00-$FF00 with $FD and sets I = $FE, so any bus value gives the vector $FDFD. In play the table is no longer needed and $D9D6 builds the shadow list at $FE64 in it (8 bytes an object), drawn by $DDE7 before the blit. docs/02-architecture.md 2.3, docs/06-rendering.md 6.6.
$FF00 b SpriteListArea 16 ; The depth list and the sprite list, then the stack
> $FF00 holds the depth list of $EFA5 (4 bytes an entry) and then, rebuilt by $D9D6 each pass, the sprite list drawn by $EAE3 (6 bytes an entry); its first byte is also the last vector byte of the IM 2 table. The stack grows down from $FFFF (SP = $FFFF at the entry, $61A8). The bytes in the image up to $FF57 are leftovers. docs/02-architecture.md 2.2.
$FF58 b UdgLeftover 8 ; The 48K BASIC's user-defined graphics, left in the image
> $FF58-$FFFF is where the Spectrum ROM keeps the UDGs, set up as copies of the letters A-U; the bytes are those copies, left from BASIC. The game never reads them; the top of the stack overwrites the last ones.
